\h Hebrews
\toc1 The Epistle of S. Paul the Apostle to the Hebrews.
\toc2 Hebrews
\toc3 HEB
\mt1 The Epistle of S. Paul the Apostle to the Hebrews.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE EPISTLE OF S. PAUL TO THE HEBREWS.
\ip THAT the Hebrewes were not all the Jews, but only a part of them, it is manifeſt Act. 6: where the primitiue Church of Hieruſalem, although it conſiſted of Jews only, as we reade Act. 2. yet is ſaid to conſiſt of two ſortes, Greekes & Hebrues. Which againe is manifeſt Phil. 3. where S. Paul comparing himſelf with the Iudaical falſe-Apoſtles, ſaith, that he alſo is, \it an Hebrue of Hebrues.\it* Finally, they ſeeme to haue been thoſe Jews which were borne in Iurie, which for the moſt part dwelled alſo there. Therefore to the Chriſtian Jews in Hieruſalem and in the reſt of Iurie, S. Paul writeth this Epiſtle, out of Italie: ſaying therevpon, \it The brethren of Italie ſalute you.\it* Heb. 13. By which wordes, & by theſe other in the ſame place, \it Know ye our brother Timothee to be dimiſſed, with whom (if he come the ſooner) I wil ſee you\it*, it is euident, that he wrote this, not only after he was brought priſoner to Rome, wherein S. Luke endeth the Actes of the Apoſtles: but alſo after he was ſet at libertie there againe.
\ip Many cauſes are giuen of the Doctors, why writing to the Jews, he doth not put his name in the beginning, \it Paul an Apoſtle &c.\it* as he doth lightly in his Epiſtles to the Churches and Biſhops of the Gentils. The moſt likely cauſe is, for that he was \it the preacher and Apoſtle and Maiſter of the\it* \f - \ft \+it 2. Tim. 1.\+it*\f* \it Gentils.\it* And againe in another place he ſaith , that himſelf was appointed the Apoſtle of the \f - \ft \+it 1. Tim. 2.\+it*\f* Gentils, as Peter of the Jews. Gal. 2. Only S. Peter therfore writing to the Jews, doth vſe this ſtile: \it Peter an Apoſtle of IESVS Chriſt &c.\it* becauſe he was more peculiarly their Apoſtle, as \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 1.\+it*\f* being the vicar of Chriſt, who was alſo himſelf more ſpecially \it the miniſter of the\it* \f - \ft * Yet was Chriſt head of the Gentils alſo. So likewiſe his vicar S. Peter, notwithſtanding his more peculiar Apoſtleſhip ouer the Jews.\f* \f - \ft \+it Rom. 5.\+it*\f* \it Circumciſion\it*, that is (as himſelf ſpeaketh) \it not ſent but to the ſheepe which were loſt of the houſe of Iſraël.\it* Mat. 15.
\ip The Argument of the Epiſtle S. Paul himſelf doth tell vs in two wordes, calling it \it verbum ſolatij,\it* \f - \ft \+it Heb. 13.\+it*\f* \it the word of ſolace and comfort.\it* Which alſo is plaine in the whole courſe of the Epiſtle, namely in the tenth chapter. v. 32. &c. Where he exhorteth them to take great comfort and confidence in their manifold tribulations ſuſtained of their owne countrie-men the Jews, whereof the Apoſtle alſo maketh mention to the Theſſalonians. 1. Theſs. 2. v. 14. Thoſe perſecutions then of the obſtinate incredulous Jews their countrie-men, was one great tentation vnto them. Another tentation was, the perſuaſions that they brought vnto them out of Scriptures, to cleaue vnto the Law, and not to beleeue in IESVS the dead man.
\ip And whereas the Jews did magnifie their law, by the Prophetes, and by the Angels by whom it was giuen, and by Moyſes, and by their land of promiſe, into which Ioſue brought them, and by their father Abraham, and by their Aaronicall or Leuitical prieſthod and ſacrifices, by their Tabernacle, & by their Teſtament: he ſheweth, that our Lord IESVS, as being the natural Sonne of God, paſſeth incomparably the Prophetes, the Angels, and Moyſes: that the Reſt or quietnes which God promiſed, was not in their earthly land, but in heauen: that his figure Melchiſedec far paſſed Abraham: and that his prieſthod, Sacrifice, Tabernacle, and teſtament, far paſſed theirs. In al which he ſhooteth often at theſe three markes: to take away the ſcandal of Chriſtes death, by giuing them ſundrie good reaſons & teſtimonies of it: to erect their mindes from viſible and earthly promiſes (to which only, the Jews were wholy bent) to inuiſible and heauenly: and to inſinuate that the Ceremonies ſhould now ceaſe, the time of their correction by Chriſt being now come. The Epiſtle may be deuided into theſe partes: the firſt, Of Chriſtes excellencie aboue the Prophetes, Angels, Moyſes, and Ioſue: c. 1. 2. 3. 4. The ſecond, of his prieſthod and excellencie thereof aboue the prieſthod of the old Teſtament: c. 5. vnto the middeſt of the 10. The laſt part is of exhortation c. 10. v. 9. to the end of the Epiſtle.
\is THE EPISTLE OF S. PAUL THE APOSTLE TO THE HEBREWS.
\ip \f - \ft Heretical corruption.\f* Let the Chriſtian Reader note the corruption and impudent boldnes of our Adverſaries, that vpon a falſe private perſuaſion of their owne, that S. Paul was not the author of this Epiſtle, \f - \ft \+it * In the Engliſh Bible of the year 1579.\+it*\f* leave out his name in the title of the ſame, contrarie to the authentical copies both Greeke and Latin. Jn old time there was ſome doubt who ſhould be the writer of it, but then, when it was no leſs doubted whether it were Canonical Scripture at all. \f - \ft The Epiſtle to the Hebrews is, S. Paul's.\f* Afterward the whole Church (by which only we know the true Scriptures from other writings) held it and deliuered it, as now ſhe doth, to the faithful, for Canonical, and for S. Paul's Epiſtle. Notwithſtanding the Adverſaries would haue refuſed the Epiſtle, as well as they do the Author, but that they falſely imagine certain places thereof to make againſt the Sacrifice of the Maſs.
\c 1
\cd God ſpake to their fathers by the Prophets: but to them ſelues by his owne Sonne, 14. who incomparably paſſeth al the Angels.
\p
\v 1 \sc DIVERSELY\sc* \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The Epiſtle at the third Maſs on Chriſtmas day.\f* and many vvaies in times paſt God ſpeaking to the fathers in the prophets:
\v 2 laſt of al in theſe daies hath ſpoken to vs in his Sonne, vvhom he hath appointed heire of al, by vvhom he made alſo the vvorldes.
\v 3 \x - \xt Sap. 7, 26.\x* Vvho being the \f 2 \fr 1.3 \ft \+it c ἀπαύγασμα\+it*\f* cbrightneſſe of his glorie, and the \f 3 \fr 1.3 \ft \+it b χαρακτὴρ ὑποστάσεως\+it*\f* bfigure of his ſubſtance, & carying al things by the vvord of his povver, making purgation of ſinnes, ſitteth on the right hand of the Maieſtie in the high places: \ef - \fr 1.3 \fq The figure. \ft To be the figure of his ſubſtance, ſignifieth nothing els but that vvhich S. Paul ſpeaketh in other vvordes to the Philippians c. 2. v. 6. that he is the forme and moſt expreſſe reſemblance of his fathers ſubſtance. So S. Ambroſe and others expound it, and the Greeke vvord ¹ \+it Character\+it* is very ſignificant to that purpoſe. ² Note alſo by this place, that the Sonne, though he be a figure of his Fathers ſubſtance, is notvvithſtanding of the ſame ſubſtance. So Chriſtes body in the Sacrament and his myſtical death and ſacrifice in the ſame, though called a figure, image, or repreſentation of Chriſtes viſible body and ſacrifice vpon the Croſſe, yet may be and is the ſelf ſame in ſubſtance. \fq ¹ \ft \+it μορφὴ χαρακτὴρ.\+it* \fq ² \ft The B. Sacrament a figure and yet the true body.\ef*
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 1.4 \ft The excellency of Chriſt above Angels.\f* being made ſo much better then Angels, as he hath inherited a more excellent name aboue them.
\v 5 For to vvhich of the Angels hath he ſaid at any time, \x - \xt Ps. 2, 7.\x* \it Thou art my ſonne, to day haue I begotten thee\it*? and againe, \x - \xt 2. Reg. 7, 14.\x* \it I wil be to him a father, and he ſhal be to me a ſonne\it*.
\v 6 And vvhen againe he bringeth in the firſt begotten into the vvorld, he ſaith, \it And \it* \x - \xt Ps. 96, 8.\x* \it let al the Angels of God adore him\it*. \ef - \fr 1.6 \fq Let al the Angels adore. \ft ¹ The Heretikes maruel that vve adore Chriſt in the B. Sacrament, vvhen they might learne by this place, that vvhereſoeuer his perſon is, there it ought to be adored both of men and Angels. And vvhere they ſay it vvas not made preſent in the Sacrament nor inſtituted to be adored, vve anſvver that no more vvas he incarnate purpoſely to be adored: but yet ſtraight vpon his deſcending from heauen, it vvas the duety both of Angels and al other creatures to adore him. \fq ¹ \ft The adoration of Christ in the B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 7 And to the Angels truely he ſaith, \x - \xt Ps. 103, 4.\x* \it He that maketh his Angels, ſpirites: and his miniſters, a flame of fire\it*.
\v 8 But to the Sonne: \x - \xt Ps. 44, 7.\x* \it Thy throne ô God for euer & euer: a rod of equitie, the rod of thy kingdom\it*.
\v 9 \it Thou haſt loued iuſtice, and hated iniquitie: therfore thee, God, thy God hath anointed vvith the oile of exultation aboue thy fellovves\it*.
\v 10 And, \x - \xt Ps. 101. 26.\x* \it Thou in the beginning ô Lord didſt found the earth: and the vvorkes of thy handes are the heauens\it*.
\v 11 \it They ſhal periſh, but thou ſhalt continue: and they ſhal al vvaxe old as a garment\it*.
\v 12 \it And as a veſture ſhalt thou chaunge them, and they ſhal be changed: but thou art the ſelf ſame, and thy yeres ſhal not faile\it*. ⊢
\v 13 But to vvhich of the Angels ſaid he at any time: \x - \xt Ps. 109, 1. 1. Cor. 15, 25.\x* \it Sit on my right hand, vntil I make thine enemies the footeſtoole of\it* thy feete?
\v 14 Are they not al, \f 1 \fr 1.14 \ft The holy Angels (ſaith S. Auguſtine) to the ſocietie of vvhom vve aſpire in this our peregrination, as they haue eternitie to continue, ſo alſo facilitie to knovv, and felicitie to reſt: for they do helpe vs vvithout al difficultie, becauſe vvith their ſpiritual motions pure and free, they labour or trauel not. \+it De Ciuit. li. 11. c. 31\+it*.\f* miniſtring ſpirits: ſent to miniſter for them vvhich ſhal receiue the inheritance of ſaluation?
\c 2
\cd He inferreth of the foreſaid, that it ſhal be incomparably more damnable for them to neglect the new Teſtament then the old, 3 conſidering the irrefragable authoritie of the Apoſtles alſo. 5 Then he proſecuteth the excellencie of Chriſt aboue the Angels, 9 who neuertheles, vvas made leſſer then Angels, to ſuffer and die for men, to deſtroy the dominion of the Diuel, 15 to deliuer men from feare of death, 17 and to be a fitte Prieſt for men.
\p
\v 1 \sc THERFORE\sc* more aboundantly ought vve to obſerue thoſe things vvhich vve haue heard: \f 1 \fr 2.1 \ft As that vvhich runneth out of a broken veſſel, or that runneth by, iſ loſt.\f*\f 2 \fr 2.1 \ft \+it μὴ ποτε παραρρυῶμεν\+it*\f* leſt perhaps vve runne out.
\v 2 For if the vvord that vvas ſpoken by Angels, became ſure, and al preuarication and diſobedience hath receiued a iuſt retribution of revvard:
\v 3 hovv ſhal we eſcape if vve neglect ſo great ſaluation? vvhich vvhen it vvas begonne to be declared by our Lord, of them that heard vvas confirmed on vs,
\v 4 \x - \xt Mar. 16, 20.\x* God vvithal teſtifying by ſignes, & vvonders, and diuers miracles, & diſtributions of the holy Ghoſt according to his vvil.
\v 5 For not to Angels hath God made ſubiect the world to come, whereof vve ſpeake.
\v 6 But one hath teſtified in a certaine place, ſaying: \x - \xt Ps. 8, 5.\x* \it VVhat is man, that thou art mindeful of him: or the ſonne of man, that thou viſiteſt him\it*?
\v 7 \it Thou didſt miniſh him litle leſſe then Angels: with glorie and honour thou haſt crowned him, and conſtituted him ouer the workes of thy handes\it*.
\v 8 \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15. Eph. 1.\x* \it Al things haſt thou made ſubiect vnder his feete\it*. For in that he ſubiected al things to him, he left nothing not ſubiect to him. But novv vve ſee not as yet al things ſubiected to him.
\v 9 But \x - \xt Philip. 2, 8.\x* him that vvas a litle leſſened vnder the Angels, vve ſee \sc IESVS\sc*, \f 2 \fr 2.9 \ft This proueth againſt the Caluiniſts that Chriſt by his Paſſiō merited his ovvne glorification. Which they vvould not for ſhame deny of Chriſt, but that they are at a point to deny al meritorious vvorkes, yea euē in Chriſt alſo. and therfore they tranſlate alſo this ſentence heretically, by tranſpoſing the vvordes. \+it In the Bible printed the yere 1579.\+it*\f* becauſe of the paſſion of death, crovvned with glorie and honour: that through the grace of God he might taſt death for al.
\v 10 For it became him for vvhom al things, and by vvhom al things, that had brought many children into glorie, to conſummate the author of their ſaluation, by his paſſion.
\v 11 For he that ſanctifieth, and they that be ſanctified: al of one. For the which cauſe he is not aſhamed to call them brethren,
\v 12 ſaying, \x - \xt Ps. 21, 23.\x* \it I vvil declare thy name to my brethren: in the middes of the Church wil I praiſe thee\it*.
\v 13 And againe, \x - \xt Ps. 17, 3.\x* \it I vvil haue affiance in him\it*. And againe, \x - \xt Eſa. 8, 18.\x* \it Behold here am I and my children: vvhom God hath giuen me\it*.
\v 14 Therfore becauſe the children haue communicated vvith fleſh and bloud, him ſelf alſo in like maner hath bene partaker of the ſame: that \x - \xt Oſe. 13, 14. 1. Cor. 15, 54.\x* by death he might deſtroy him that had the empire of death, that is to ſay, the Deuil:
\v 15 and might deliuer them that by the feare of death through al their life vvere ſubiect to ſeruitude.
\v 16 For no vvhere doth he take Angels: but \f 1 \fr 2.16 \ft The dignitie of man, in that Chriſt tooke our nature vnto his perſon in Deitie, and not the nature of Angels.\f* the cſeede of Abraham he taketh.
\v 17 VVherevpon he ought in al things to be like vnto his brethren: that he might become a merciful and faithful high Prieſt before God, that he might repropitiate the ſinnes of the people.
\v 18 For in that vvherein him ſelf ſuffered and vvas tempted: he is able to helpe them alſo that are tempted.
\c 3
\cd By example of Chriſt (who is incomparably more excellent then Moyſes alſo) he exhorteth them to be faithful vnto God. 7 Their reward ſhal be to enter into euerlaſting reſt, if they perſeuere, as contrariwiſe to be excluded (as was ſhadowed in their forefathers in the wildernes) if they ſinne and become incredulous.
\p
\v 1 \sc WHEREFORE\sc* holy brethren, partakers of the heauenly vocation, conſider the Apoſtle, & high prieſt of our confeſſion \sc IESVS\sc*:
\v 2 vvho is faithful to him that made him, as alſo \x - \xt Nu. 12, 7.\x* Moyſes in al his houſe.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 3.3 \ft The excellencie of Chriſt aboue Moyſes.\f* For, this man is eſteemed vvorthie of more ample glorie aboue Moyſes, by ſo much as more ample glorie then the houſe, hath he that framed it.
\v 4 For euery houſe is framed of ſome man. but he that created al things, is God.
\v 5 And Moyſes in deede vvas faithful in al his houſe as a ſeruant, for a teſtimonie of thoſe things vvhich vvere to be ſaid:
\v 6 but Chriſt as the Sonne in his ovvne houſe: which houſe are vve, if vve keepe firme the confidence and glorie of hope vnto the end.
\v 7 Vvherefore, as the holy Ghoſt ſaith, \x - \xt Ps. 94, 8.\x* \it To day if you ſhal heare his voice\it*,
\v 8 \it harden not your hartes as in the exacerbation according to the day of tentation in the deſert\it*,
\v 9 \it vvhere your fathers tempted me: proued & ſaw my vvorkes\it*
\v 10 \it fourtie yeres. For the which cauſe I was offended vvith this generation, and ſaid, They doe alvvaies erre in hart. And they haue not knowen my waies\it*.
\v 11 \it to whom I ſvvare in my vvrath, If they ſhal enter into my reſt\it*.
\v 12 Bevvare brethren, leſt perhaps there be in ſome of you an euil hart of incredulitie, to depart from the liuing God.
\v 13 but exhort your ſelues euery day, vvhiles to day is named, that none of you be obdurate vvith the fallacie of ſinne,
\v 14 For vve be made partakers of Chriſt: yet ſo if vve keepe the \f 1 \fr 3.14 \ft Faith is the groundworke of our creation in Chriſt, which if we hold not faſt, al the building is loſt.\f* beginning of his ſubſtance firme vnto the end.
\v 15 VVhile it is ſaid, \it To day if you ſhal heare his voice, do not obdurate your hartes as in that exacerbation\it*.
\v 16 For ſome hearing did exaſperate: but not al they that vvent out of Ægypt by Moyſes.
\v 17 And vvith vvhom vvas he offended fourtie yeres? vvas it not with them that ſinned, \x - \xt Nu. 14, 37. 21, 23.\x* vvhoſe carcaſſes vvere ouerthrovven in the deſert?
\v 18 And to vvhom did he ſvveare that they ſhould not enter into his reſt: but to them that were incredulous?
\v 19 And vve ſee that they could not enter in, becauſe of incredulitie.
\c 4
\cd That they muſt feare to be excluded out of the foreſaid reſt (which he proueth out of the pſalme) 12 conſidering that Chriſt ſeeth their moſt invvard ſecretes. 14 And that he (as their Prieſt who alſo him ſelf ſuffered) is able and ready to ſtrengthen them in confeſſion of their faith.
\p
\v 1 \sc LET\sc* vs feare therfore leſt perhaps forſaking the promis of entring into his reſt, ſome of you be thought to be vvanting.
\v 2 For to vs alſo it hath been denounced, as alſo to them. but the vvord of hearing did not profit them, not mixt vvith faith of thoſe things vvhich they heard.
\v 3 For vve that haue beleeued, ſhal enter into the reſt: as he ſaid, \x - \xt Ps. 94, 11.\x* \it As I ſvvare in my vvrath, if they ſhal enter into my reſt\it*: and truely the vvorkes from the foundation of the vvorld being perfited.
\v 4 For he ſaid in a certaine place of the \x - \xt Gen. 2, 2.\x* ſeuenth day, thus: \f 2 \fr 4.4 \ft If the Apoſtle had not euidently here ſhewed that the Sabboths reſt vvas a figure of the eternal repoſe in heauen, who durſt to haue applied that Scripture of Gods reſt the ſeuenth day, to that purpoſe? Or hovv can our Aduerſaries novv reprehend the like applicatiō manifoldly vſed in al holy auncient vvriters to the like end?\f* \it And God reſted the ſeuenth day from al his vvorkes\it*.
\v 5 And againe in this, \it If they ſhal enter into my reſt\it*.
\v 6 Becauſe then it remaineth that certaine enter into it, and they to vvhom firſt it vvas preached, did not enter becauſe of incredulitie:
\v 7 Againe he limiteth a certaine day: \x - \xt Heb. 3, 7.\x* \it To day\it*, in Dauid ſaying, after ſo long time, as is aboue ſaid, \it To day if you ſhal heare his voice: doe not obdurate your hartes\it*.
\v 8 For if \f 1 \fr 4.8 \ft c So Ioſue is called in Greeke.\f* Ieſus had giuen them reſt: he vvould neuer ſpeake of an other day aftervvard.
\v 9 Therfore there is left a ſabbatiſme for the people of God.
\v 10 For he that is entred into his reſt, the ſame alſo hath reſted from his vvorkes, as God from his.
\v 11 Let vs haſten therfore to enter into that reſt: that no man fal into the ſame example of incredulitie.
\v 12 For \f 1 \fr 4.12 \ft the word of God. Whatſoever God threateneth by his word concerning the puniſhment of ſin and incredulity, ſhall be executed, be the offence never ſo ſecret, deep, or hidden in our hearts: becauſe God's ſpeech paſſeth eaſily and ſearcheth throughly every part, power, and faculty of man's ſoul.\f* the vvord of God is liuely and forcible, and more perſing then any tvvo edged ſword: and reaching vnto the diuiſion of the ſoule and the ſpirit, of the ioyntes alſo and the marowes, and a diſcerner of the cogitations and intentes of the hart.
\v 13 And there is no creature inuiſible in his ſight. but al things are naked and open to his eies, to vvhom our ſpeache is.
\v 14 Hauing therfore a great high Prieſt that hath entred the heauens, \sc IESVS\sc* the ſonne of God, let vs hold the confeſſion.
\v 15 For we haue not a high prieſt that can not haue compaſſion on our infirmities: but tempted in al things by ſimilitude, except ſinne.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 4.16 \ft The Epiſtle in a Maſſe for the election of the Pope.\f* Let vs goe therfore vvith confidence to the throne of grace: that vve may obteine mercie, and finde grace in ſeaſonable aide. \ef - \fr 4.16 \fq Let vs go with confidence. \ft The Aduerſaries go about to proue by theſe wordes that vve neede no helpe of Saincts to obtaine any thing, Chriſt him ſelf being ſo readie, and we being admoniſhed to come to him with confidence as to a moſt merciful Mediator and Biſhop. ¹ But by that argument they may as wel take away the helpes and praiers of the liuing one for an other. And we do not require the helpe either of the Saincts in heauen, or of our brethren in earth, for any miſtruſt of Gods mercie, but for our owne vnworthines: being aſſured that the praier of a iuſt man auaileth more with him, then the deſire of a greuous ſinner: and of a number making interceſſion together, rather then of a man alone. vvhich the Heretikes can not deny except they reproue the plaine Scriptures. Neither do vve come leſſe to him, or vvith leſſe confidence, vvhen vve come accompanied with the praiers of Angels, Saincts, Prieſts, or iuſt men ioyning vvith vs, as they fondly imagine and pretend: but vvith much more affiance in his grace, mercie, and merites, then if we praied our ſelues alone. \fq ¹ \ft Scripture abused against inuocation of Saincts.\ef*
\c 5
\cd That Chriſt being a man and infirme, vvas therein but as al Prieſts. and that he alſo was called of God to this office: offering as the others: 8 and ſuffered obediently for our example. 11 Of whoſe Prieſthod he hath much to ſay, but that the Hebrues haue neede rather to heare their Catechiſme againe.
\p
\v 1 \sc FOR\sc* \f 1 \fr 5.1 \ft The Epiſtle upon the feaſt of S. Thomas of Canterbury. Decem. 29. And in his Tranſlation Iulij. 7. And for a Biſhop that is a Confeſſor.\f* euery high Prieſt taken from among men, is appointed for men in thoſe things that pertaine to God: that he may offer giftes and ſacrifices for ſinnes: \ef - \fr 5.1 \fq Euery high Prieſt. \ft ¹ By the deſcription of a Prieſt or high Prieſt (for to this purpoſe al is one matter) he proueth Chriſt to be one in moſt excellent ſort. Firſt then, a Prieſt muſt not be an Angel, or of any other nature but mans. Secondly, euery man is not a Prieſt, but ſuch an one as is ſpecially choſen out of the reſt, and preferred before other of the community, ſeuered, aſſumpted, and exalted into a higher ſtate and dignitie then the vulgar. Thirdly, the cauſe and purpoſe why he is ſo ſequeſtred and piked out from the reſidue, is to take charge of Diuine things, to deale as a mediator betvvixt God and the people, to be the Deputie of men in ſuch things as they haue to craue or to receiue of God, and to preſent or giue to him againe. Fourthly, the moſt proper and principal part of a Prieſts office is, to offer oblations, giftes, and ſacrifices to God for the ſinnes of the people: vvithout vvhich kind of moſt ſoueraine dueties, no perſon, people, or Commonwealth can appertaine to God: and which can be done by none, of what other dignitie or calling ſoeuer he be in the vvorld, that is not a Prieſt: diuers Princes (as vve read in the Scriptures) puniſhed by God, and king Saul depoſed from his kingdom, ſpecially for attempting the ſame. ² ³ And generally vve may learne here, that \+it in ys quæ ſunt ad Deum\+it*, in all matters touching God, his ſeruice, and religion, the Prieſt hath onely charge and authority: as the Prince temporal is the peoples gouernour, guider, and ſoueraine, in the things touching their worldly affaires: Vvhich muſt for al that by him be directed and manneged no otherwiſe, but as is agreable to the due worſhip and ſeruice of God. againſt which if the terrene Powers commit any thing, the Prieſts ought to admoniſh them from God. ⁴ We learne alſo hereby, that euery one is not a Prieſt, and that the people muſt alvvaies haue certaine perſons choſen out from among them, to deale in their ſutes and cauſes vvith God, to pray, to miniſter Sacraments, and to ſacrifice for them. ⁵ And vvhereas the Proteſtants vvil haue no Prieſt, Prieſthod, nor ſacrifice, but Chriſt and his death, pretending theſe vvordes of the Apoſtle to be verified onely in the Prieſthod and Seruice of the old lavv, and Chriſtes perſon alone, and after him of no moe: therein they ſhew them ſelues to be ignorant of the Scriptures, and of the ſtate of the nevv Teſtament, and induce a plaine Atheiſme and Godleſneſſe into the vvorld. for, ſo long as man hath to doe with God, there muſt needes be ſome deputed, and choſen out from among the reſt, to deale according to this declaration of the Apoſtle, in things pertaining to God, and thoſe muſt be Prieſts. for els, if men neede to deale no more, but immediatly with Chriſt, vvhat doe they vvith their Miniſters? Vvhy let they not euery man pray, and miniſter for him ſelf, and to him ſelf? What doe they vvith Sacraments, ſeing Chriſtes death is as wel ſufficient vvithout them, as vvithout ſacrifice? Vvhy ſtandeth not his death as vvel vvith Sacrifice, as vvith Sacraments: as vvel vvith Prieſthod, as vvith other Eccleſiaſtical function? There is no other cauſe in the world, but that (Sacrifice being the moſt principal act of religion that man oweth to God, both by his Law, and by the lavv of nature) the Diuel by theſe his miniſters, vnder pretence of deferring or attributing the more to Chriſtes death, would aboliſh it. ⁶ This definition of a Prieſt and his function, vvith al the properties thereto belonging, holdeth not onely in the law of Moyſes, and order of Aarons Prieſthod, but it was true before, in the law of nature, in the Patriarches, in Melchiſedec, and now in Chriſt, and all his Apoſtles, and Prieſts of the new Teſtament: ſauing that it is a peculiar excellencie in Chriſt, that he onely offered for other mens ſinnes, and not at all for his ovvne, as all other doe. \fq ¹ \ft The description of a Priest, and his office. \fq ² \ft \+it 3 Reg. 13. 2 Par. 26. 1 Reg. 13\+it*. \fq ³ \ft The Princes temporal authoritie how far it extendeth. \fq ⁴ \ft There is a peculiar order & calling of Priests of the new Testament. \fq ⁵ \ft Priests and sacrifice necessarie in the new Testament, & nothing derogatorie to Christs priesthod or sacrifice. \fq ⁶ \ft The dignity & function of Priesthood is not to be usurped.\ef*
\v 2 that cā haue compaſſion on them that be ignorant and do erre: becauſe him ſelf alſo is compaſſed vvith infirmitie:
\v 3 & therfore he ought, as for the people, ſo alſo for him ſelf to offer for ſinnes.
\v 4 \x - \xt 2. Par. 26, 18.\x* Neither doth any man take the honour to him ſelf, but he that is called of God, \x - \xt 1. Par. 23. 13.\x* as Aaron. ⊢ \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq Taketh to him ſelf. \ft ¹ A ſpecial prouiſo for all Prieſts, preachers, and ſuch as haue to deale for the people in things pertaining to God, that they take not that honour or office at their ovvne hands, but by lawful calling and conſecration, euen as Aaron did. By which clauſe if you examine Luther, Caluin, Beza, and the like, or if al ſuch as novv a daies intrude them ſelues into ſacred functions, looke into their conſciences, great and foul matter of damnation wil appeare. \fq ¹ \ft The difference & excellency of Christ's Priesthood.\ef*
\v 5 So Chriſt alſo did not glorifie him ſelf that he might be made a high prieſt: but he that ſpake to him, \x - \xt Pſ. 2, 7.\x* \it My Sonne art thou, I this day haue begotten thee\it*. \ef - \fr 5.5 \fq Did not glorifie him ſelf. \ft ¹ The dignity of Prieſthod muſt needes be paſſing high and ſoueraine, when it was a promotion and preferment in the ſonne of God him ſelf according to his manhod, and when he would not vſurpe, nor take vpon him the ſame, vvithout his fathers expreſſe commiſſion and calling therevnto. An eternal example of humility, and an argument of condemnation to al mortal men, that arrogate vniuſtly any function or povver ſpiritual, that is not giuen them from aboue, and by lawful calling, and commiſſion of their ſuperiors. \fq ¹ \ft All true priests and preachers must be lawfully called thereto.\ef*
\v 6 As alſo in an other place he ſaith, \x - \xt Pſ. 109, 4.\x* \it Thou art a prieſt for euer, according to the order of Melchiſedec\it*. ⊢ \ef - \fr 5.6 \fq A Prieſt for euer. \ft ¹ In the 109 Pſalme, from vvhence this teſtimonie is taken, both Chriſtes kingdom and Prieſthod are ſet fourth. but the Apoſtle vrgeth ſpecially his Prieſthod, as the more excellent and preeminent ſtate in him, our Redemption being vvrought & atchieued by ſacrifice, vvhich was an act of his Prieſthod, and not of his kingly povver. though he was properly a king alſo, as Melchiſedec was both Prieſt, and king, being a reſemblance of Chriſt in both, but much more in his Prieſthod. And our Lord had this excellent double dignitie (as appeareth by the diſcourſe of S. Paul, and his allegations here out of the Pſalmes) ² at the very firſt moment of his conception or incarnation. ³ for you muſt bevvare of the wicked hereſie of the Arians and Caluiniſts (except in theſe later it be rather an errour proceding of ignorance) that ſticke not to ſay, that Chriſt vvas a Prieſt, or did ſacrifice, according to his Godhead. Which is to make Chriſt, God the fathers Prieſt, and not his ſonne, and to doe ſacrifice and homage to him as his Lord, and not as his equal in dignity and nature. Therfore S. Auguſtine ſaith \+it in Pſal. 109. That as he vvas man, he was Prieſt: as God, he vvas not Prieſt\+it*. And Theodorete in Pſal 109. \+it As man, he did offer ſacrifice: but as God, he did receiue ſacrifice\+it*. And againe, \+it Chriſt touching his humanity was called a Prieſt, and he offered no other hoſt but his owne body. &c\+it*. Dialog. 1 circa med. ⁴ Some of our new Maiſters not knovving ſo much, did let fall out of their pennes the contrarie, and being admoniſhed of the errour, and that it was very Arianiſme, yet they perſiſt in it of mere ignorance in the groundes of Diuinitie. \fq ¹ \ft Christ both Priest & king: but his Priesthod more excellent of the two. \fq ² \ft Christ a Priest as he is man, not as he is God. \fq ³ \ft \+it Psal. c. 109.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Retent. pag. 89\+it*.\ef*
\v 7 VVho in the daies of his fleſh, vvith a ſtrong crie and teares, offering praiers and ſupplications to him that could ſaue him from death, vvas heard for his reuerence. ⊢ \ef - \fr 5.7 \fq With a ſtrong crie. \ft ¹ Though our Sauiour make interceſſion for vs, according to his humane nature, continually in heauen alſo: yet he doth not in any external creatures make ſacrifice, nor vſe the praiers ſacrificall, by which our redemption was atchieued, as he did in the time of his mortal life, and in the act of his Paſſion, and moſt principally, vvhen vvith a loud voice, and vvith this praier, ² \+it In manus tuas commendo ſpiritum meum\+it*, he voluntarily depoſed his ſoul, yelding it in moſt proper ſort for a ſacrifice. for in that laſt point of his death, conſiſteth ſpecially his high Prieſtly office, and the very vvorke and conſummation of our redemption. ³ Obſerue more ouer, that though commonly euery faithful perſon pray both for him ſelf and others, and offer his praiers to God, yet none offereth by office, and ſpecial deputation, and appointment, in the perſon of the whole Church and people, ſauing the Prieſt. Vvhoſe praiers therfore be more effectual in them ſelues, for that they be the voice of all faithful men together, made by him that is appointed and receiued of God for the peoples legate. ⁴ And of this kind vvere all Chriſtes praiers, in all his life and death, as all his other actions were: his faſting, vvatching, preaching, inſtituting, miniſtring, or receiuing Sacraments: euery one being done as Prieſtly actions. \fq ¹ \ft The sacrifice on the Crosse, was the principal acte of Christs priesthod. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 23, 46\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Priests praiers more effectual. \fq ⁴ \ft Christs Priestly actions.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.7 \fq For his reuerence. \ft ¹ Theſe vvordes haue our Engliſh Tranſlators perniciouſly and moſt preſumptuouſly corrupted, turning them thus, \+it In that which he feared\+it*, contrarie to the verſion and ſenſe of al antiquity, and to Eraſmus alſo, and contrarie to the ordinarie vſe of the Greeke word, ²as Beza him ſelf defineth it \+it Luc. 2 v. 25\+it*: and contrarie to the propriety of the Greeke phraſe, as not onely the Catholikes, but ³ the beſt learned Lutherans do ſhew and proue by many examples. They folow herein the ſingular preſumption of Caluin, vvho was the firſt (as his fellow Beza confeſſeth) that euer found out this interpretation. Vvhich neither S. Chryſoſtom, nor any other, as perfect Græcians as they vvere, could euer eſpie. Vvhere, onely to haue made choiſe of that impious and arrogant Sectaries ſenſe, before the ſaid fathers and all the Churches beſides, had been ſhameful ynough: but to ſet the ſame downe for very Scripture of Gods bleſſed word, that is intolerable, and paſſeth al impiety. And we ſee plainely that they haue no conſcience, indifferencie, nor other purpoſe, but to make the poore Readers beleeue, that their opinions be Gods ovvne vvord, and to draw the Scriptures to ſound after the fantaſie of their hereſies. But if the good Reader knew, for what point of doctrine they haue thus framed their tranſlation, ⁴ they would abhorre them to the depth of Hel. forſooth it is thus: they vvould haue this Scripture meane, that Chriſt was in horrible feare of damnation, and that he vvas not onely in paines corporal vpon the Croſſe (vvhich they hold, not to haue been ſufficient for mans redemption) but that he was in the very ſorovves & diſtreſſes of the damned, without any difference, but that it vvas not euerlaſting, as theirs is. For this horrible blaſphemie (vvhich is their interpretation of Chriſtes deſcending into Hel) Gods holy word muſt be corrupted, and the ſacrifice of Chriſtes death (vvhereof they talke ſo preſumptuouſly) muſt not be ynough for our redemption, except he be damned for vs alſo to the paines of Hel. Wo be to our poore Countrie, that muſt haue ſuch bookes, and read ſuch tranſlations. See Caluin and Beza in their Commentaries and Annotations vpon this place, and you ſhal ſee, that for defenſe of the ſaid blaſphemies they haue thus tranſlated this text. See the Annotations before, \+it Act. 2, 27\+it*. and \+it Mat. 27, 46\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Notorious Heretical translation to mainteine Caluins horrible blasphemie. \fq ² \ft \+it ἀπὸ εὐλαβείας\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Flac. Illyr. vpon this place\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft Caluins blasphemie that Christ suffered hel paines vpon the crosse, and that his death otherwise were insufficient.\ef*
\v 8 And truely vvhereas he was the Sonne, he learned by thoſe things vvhich he ſuffered, obedience:
\v 9 and being conſummate, was made to al that obey him, cauſe of eternal ſaluation, \ef - \fr 5.9 \fq Conſummate. \ft ¹ The ful worke of his ſacrifice, by vvhich vve vvere redeemed, vvas vvholy conſummate and accompliſhed, at the yelding vp of his ſpirit to God the Father, vvhen he ſaid, ² \+it Conſummatum eſt\+it*. though for to make the ſame effectual to the ſaluation of particular men, he him ſelf did diuers things, and now doth in heauen, and our ſelues alſo muſt vſe many meanes, for the application thereof to our particular neceſſities. See the next Annotation. \fq ¹ \ft Christ yelding vp the Ghost, accomplished our redemption. \fq ² \ft \+it Io. 19. 30.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 5.9 \fq Was made to all. \ft ¹ The Proteſtants vpon pretence of the ſufficiencie of Chriſtes Paſſion, and his onely redemption, oppoſe them ſelues guilefully in the ſight of the ſimple, againſt the inuocation of Saincts, and their interceſſion, and help of vs, againſt our penitential workes or ſuffering for our owne ſinnes, either in this life or the next: againſt the merites of faſting, praying, almes, and other things commended to vs in holy Vvritte, and againſt moſt things done in the Church, in ſacrifice, Sacrament, and ceremonie. But this place and many other ſhew, that Chriſtes Paſſion, though it be of it ſelf far more ſufficient and forcible, then the Proteſtants in their baſeneſſe of vnderſtanding can conſider, yet profiteth none but ſuch, as both doe his commaundements, and vſe ſuch remedies and meanes to apply the benefite thereof to them ſelues, as he appointeth in his word, or by the Holy Ghoſt in his Church. And the Heretikes that ſay, faith onely is the thing required to apply Chriſtes benefites vnto vs, are hereby alſo eaſily refuted. for vve do not obey him onely by beleeuing, but by doing whatſoeuer he commaundeth. Laſtly, vve note in the ſame wordes, that Chriſt appointeth not by his abſolute and eternal election, men ſo to be partakers of the fruite of his redemption, vvithout any condition or reſpect of their ovvne vvorkes, obedience, or free wil: but vvith this condition alvvaies, if men vvil obey him, and do that which he appointeth. See S. Auguſtine ( or Proſper ) to. 7 Reſponſ. Proſperi \+it li. 2. articulo 1 ad obiectiones Vincentij\+it*, where he ſaith of the cup of Chriſtes paſſion, \+it It hath in deede in it ſelf, to profite al: but if it be not drunken, it healeth not\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Christs Passion sufficient for al, but profitable to them only which obey, not by faith only, but by doing as he and his Church commaund.\ef*
\v 10 called of God a high prieſt according to the order of Melchiſedec.
\v 11 Of vvhome vve haue great ſpeache and inexplicable to vtter: becauſe you are become vveake to heare. \ef - \fr 5.11 \fq Inexplicable. \ft ¹ Intending to treate more largely and particularly of Chriſtes or Melchiſedeks Prieſthod, he forvvarneth them that the myſterie thereof is far paſſing their capacitie, and that through their feeblenes in faith and vveakenes of vnderſtanding, he is forced to omit diuers deepe points concerning the Prieſthod of the new law. Among which (no doubt) the myſterie of the Sacrament and Sacrifice of the altar, called \+sc MASSE\+sc*, was a principal & pertinent matter: which the Apoſtles and Fathers of the primitiue Church vſed not to treate of ſo largely and particularly in their writings, vvhich might come to the hands of the vnfaithful, vvho of al things tooke ſooneſt ſcandal of the B. Sacrament, as vve ſee Io. 6. \+it He ſpake to the Hebrues\+it* (ſaith S. Hierom ep 126) \+it that is, to the Iewes, and not to faithful men, to whom he might haue been bold to vtter the Sacrament\+it*. And in deede it vvas not reaſonable to talke much to them of that ſacrifice vvhich vvas the reſemblance of Chriſtes death, vvhen they thought not right of Chriſtes death it ſelf. Vvhich the Apoſtles vviſedom and ſilence our Aduerſaries wickedly abuſe againſt the holy Maſſe. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle omitteth to speake of the B. Sacrament as a mysterie then to deepe for the Iewes capacitie.\ef*
\v 12 For vvhereas you ought to be maiſters for your time, you neede to be taught againe your ſelues vvhat be the elements of the beginning of the vvordes of God: and you are become ſuch as haue neede of milke, & not of ſtrong meate.
\v 13 For euery one that is partaker of milke, is vnſkilful of the vvord of iuſtice: for he is a childe.
\v 14 But ſtrong meate is for the perfect, them that by cuſtome haue their ſenſes exerciſed to the diſcerning of good & euil.
\c 6
\cd He exhorteth them to be perfect ſcholers, and not to neede to be Catechumens againe, 4 conſidering they can not be baptized againe: 9 and remembring their former good vvorkes, for the which God vvil not faile to performe them his promis, if they faile not to imitate Abraham by perſeuerance in the faith with patience. 20 And ſo endeth his digreſſion, and returneth to the matter of Chriſtes Prieſthod.
\p
\v 1 \sc VVHERFORE\sc* intermitting the vvord of the beginning of Chriſt, let vs proceede to perfection, not againe laying the foundation of penance from dead vvorkes, and of faith tovvard God, \ef - \fr 6.1 \fq The foundation of penance. \ft ¹ Vve ſee hereby, vvhat the firſt groundes of Chriſtian inſtitution or Catechiſme were in the primitiue Church, and that there vvas euer a neceſſarie inſtruction and beleefe of certaine points had by word of mouth and tradition, before men came to the Scriptures: which could not treate of things ſo particularly, as vvas requiſite for the teaching of al neceſſarie groundes. Among theſe points were the 12 Articles conteined in the Apoſtles Creede: the doctrine of penance before Baptiſme: the maner and neceſsitie of Baptiſme: the Sacrament of Impoſition of hands after Baptiſme, called Confirmation: the articles of the Reſurrection, Iudgement, and ſuch like. Vvithout which things firſt laid, if one ſhould be ſent to picke his faith out of the Scripture, there would be madde rule quickly. See S. Auguſtine \+it in expoſt. inchoat. ep. ad Ro. verſus finem\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostles forme of Catechisme, and the points therof.\ef*
\v 2 of the doctrine of baptiſmes, and of impoſition of handes, and of the reſurrection of the dead, and of eternal iudgement.
\v 3 And this ſhal we doe, if God vvill permit.
\v 4 For \x - \xt Heb. 10, 26.\x* it is impoſſible for them that were once illuminated, haue taſted alſo the heauenly gift, and vvere made partakers of the holy Ghoſt, \ef - \fr 6.4 \fq Impoſſible. \ft ¹ How hard the holy Scriptures be, and how dangerouſly they be read of the vnlearned, or of the proud be they neuer ſo wel learned, this one place might teach vs. ² Wherat the Nouatians of old did ſo ſtumble, that they thought, and heretically taught, that none falling into any mortal ſinne after Baptiſme, could be receiued to mercie or penance in the Church: and ſo to a contentious man, that would folow his owne ſenſe, or the bare wordes, vvithout regard of the Churches ſenſe and rule of faith (after which euery Scripture muſt be expounded) the Apoſtles ſpeach doth here ſound. ³ Euen as to the ſimple, and to the Heretike that ſubmitteth not his ſenſe to the Churches iudgement, certaine places of this ſame Epiſtle, ſeeme at the firſt ſight, to ſtand againſt the daily oblation or ſacrifice of the Maſſe: which yet in truth make no more for that purpoſe, then this text we now ſtand on, ſerueth the Nouatians: as when we come to the places, it ſhal be declared. And let the good Readers beware here alſo of the Proteſtants expoſition, for they are herein worſe then Nouatians, ſpecially ſuch as preciſely folow Caluin: ⁴ holding impiouſly, that it is impoſſible for one that forſaketh entirely his faith, that is, becommeth an Apoſtata or an Heretike, to be receiued to penance or to Gods mercie. To eſtabliſh vvhich falſe and damnable ſenſe, theſe fellowes make nothing of S. Ambroſes, S. Chryſoſtoms, and the other fathers expoſition, ⁵which is the holy Churches ſenſe, ⁶ That the Apoſtle meaneth of that penance which is done before and in Baptiſme. which is no more to ſay, but that it is impoſſible to be baptized againe, and thereby to be renouated and illuminated, to die, be buried, and riſe againe the ſecond time in Chriſt, in ſo eaſie and perfect penance and cleanſing of ſinnes, as that firſt ſacrament of generation did yeld: which applieth Chriſtes death in ſuch ample maner to the receiuers, that it taketh avvay al paines due for ſinnes before committed: and therfore requireth no further penance afterward, for the ſinnes before committed, al being vvaſhed away by the force of that Sacrament duely taken. S. Auguſtine calleth the remiſsion in Baptiſme, \+it Magnam indulgentiam\+it*, a great pardon. \+it Enchirid. c. 64\+it*. ⁷ The Apoſtle therfore warneth them, that if they fall from their faith, and from Chriſtes grace and lavv which they once receiued in their Baptiſme, they may not looke to haue any more that firſt great and large remedie applied vnto them, nor no man els that ſinneth after Baptiſme: though the other penance, which is called the \+it Second table after ſinners ſhipwracke\+it*, ⁸which is a more paineful medicine for ſinne then Baptiſme, requiring much faſting, praying, and other afflictions corporal, is open not onely to other ſinners, but to al once baptized, Heretikes, or oppugners of the truth malitiouſly and of purpoſe or what way ſo euer, during this life. See S. Cyprian \+it ep. 52\+it*. S. Ambroſe vpon this place. S. Auguſtine \+it cont. ep. Parm. li. 2. c. 13\+it*. and \+it ep. 50\+it*. S. Damaſcene \+it li. 4. c. 10\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Nouatians (as al Heretikes) made Scripture the ground of their heresie. \fq ² \ft \+it Ambr. de poenit. li. 2. c. 2\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Other places make no more for the Protestants then this doth for Nouatus. \fq ⁴ \ft Caluins heresie vpon this place, worse then the Nouatians. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Ambr. loco cit. & in ep. ad Heb. Chry. ho. 9 in c. 6 ad Hebr\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft The fathers exposition of this place. \fq ⁷ \ft The Sacrament of penance is ready for al sinners whatsoeuer. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Hiero. ep. 8 ad Demetriad. c. 6\+it*.\ef*
\v 5 haue moreouer taſted the good vvord of God, and the povvers of the world to come,
\v 6 and are fallen: to be reneued againe to penance, crucifying againe to them ſelues the ſonne of God, and making him a mockerie.
\v 7 For the earth drinking the raine often comming vpon it, & bringing forth graſſe commodious for them by vvhom it is tilled, receiueth bleſſing of God.
\v 8 but bringing forth thornes and bryers, it is reprobate, and very neere a curſe, vvhoſe end is, to be burnt.
\v 9 But \f 1 \fr 6.9 \ft It is euident by theſe wordes, againſt the Nouatians and the Caluiniſts, that S. Paul meant not preciſely, that they had done, or could do any ſuch ſinne, whereby they ſhould be put out of all hope of ſaluation, and be ſure of damnation, during their life.\f* vve confidently truſt of you, my beſt beloued, better things and neerer to ſaluation: although we ſpeake thus.
\v 10 For God is not vniuſt, that he ſhould forget your vvorke & loue which you haue ſhevved in his name, vvhich haue miniſtred to the ſainctes and do miniſter. \ef - \fr 6.10 \fq God is not vniuſt. \ft ¹ It is a world to ſee, vvhat wringing & writhing the Proteſtants make, to ſhift them ſelues from the euidence of theſe wordes, which make it moſt cleere to all not blinded in pride and contention, that good workes be meritorious, and the very cauſe of ſaluation, ſo far, that God ſhould be vniuſt, if he rendered not heauen for the ſame. \+it Reuera grandis iniuſtitia Dei\+it* (ſaith S. Hierom) \+it Si tantum peccata puniret, & bona opera non ſuſciperet\+it*. That is, \+it In deede great vvere Gods iniuſtice, if he vvould onely puniſh ſinnes, and vvould not receiue good workes. Li. 2. cont. Iouin. c. 2\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Gods iustice in rewarding meritorious workes.\ef*
\v 11 And our deſire is that euery one of you ſhevv forth the ſame carefulneſſe to the accompliſhing of hope vnto the end:
\v 12 that you become not ſlouthful, but imitatours of them vvhich by faith and patience ſhal inherite the promiſſes.
\v 13 For God promiſing to Abraham, becauſe he had none greater by whom he might ſweare, he ſware by him ſelf,
\v 14 ſaying, \x - \xt Gen. 22, 16.\x* Vnles bleſſing I ſhal bleſſe thee, and multiplying ſhal multiplie thee.
\v 15 And ſo patiently enduring he obtained the promiſe.
\v 16 For men ſvveare by a greater then them ſelues: and the end of al their controuerſie, for the confirmation, is an othe.
\v 17 VVherein God meaning more aboundantly to ſhevv to the heires of the promiſe the ſtabilitie of his counſel, he interpoſed an othe:
\v 18 that by tvvo things vnmoueable, vvhereby it is impoſſible for God to lie, vve may haue a moſt ſtrong comfort. vvho haue fled to hold faſt the hope propoſed,
\v 19 vvhich vve haue as an anker of the ſoule, ſure and firme, and going in into the inner partes of the vele,
\v 20 vvhere \sc IESVS\sc* the precurſor for vs is entered, made a high prieſt for euer according to the order of Melchiſedec.
\c 7
\cd To proue the Prieſthod of Chriſt incomparably to excel the prieſthod of Aaron (and therfore, that Leuitical prieſthod now to ceaſe, and that lavv alſo with it) he ſcanneth euery vvord of the verſe alleaged out of the Pſalme, Our Lord hath ſworne: thou art a Prieſt for euer, according to the order of Melchiſedec.
\p
\v 1 \sc FOR\sc* this Melchiſedec, the king of Salem, Prieſt of the God moſt high, \x - \xt Gen. 14, 18.\x* vvho mette Abraham returning from the ſlaughter of the kings, and bleſſed him: \ef - \fr 7.1 \fq Melchiſedec. \ft The excellencie of this perſon vvas ſo great, that ſome of the antiquity tooke him to be an Angel, and ſome the holy Ghoſt. Vvhich opinion not onely the Hebrues, that auouch him to be Sem the ſonne of Noe, but alſo the cheefe fathers of the Chriſtians do condemne: not doubting but he was a mere man and a Prieſt and a king, vvhoſoeuer he vvas. for els he could not in office and order and ſacrifice haue been ſo perfect a type and reſemblance of our Sauiour, as in this Chapter and other is ſhewed.\ef*
\v 2 to vvhom alſo Abraham deuided tithes of al: firſt in deede by interpretation, \f 1 \fr 7.2 \ft ∷ When the fathers & catholike expoſitours pike out allegories and myſteries out of the names of men, the Proteſtants not endued with the Spirit vvherby the ſcriptures were giuen, deride their holy labours in the ſearch of the ſame: but the Apoſtle findeth high myſterie in the very names of perſons and places, as you ſee.\f* the king of iuſtice: & then alſo king of Salem, which is to ſay, king of peace,
\v 3 vvithout father, without mother, vvithout genealogie, hauing neither beginning of daies nor end of life, but likened to the ſonne of God, continueth a prieſt for euer. \ef - \fr 7.3 \fq Without father. \ft ¹ Not that he was vvithout father and mother, ſaith S. Hierom \+it ep. 126\+it*: for Chriſt him ſelf was not vvithout father, according to his diuinity, nor vvithout mother, in his humanity: but for that his petigree is not ſet out in the Geneſis; as the genealogie of other Patriarches is, but is ſodenly induced in the holy hiſtorie, no mention made of his ſtocke, tribe, beginning, or ending, and therfore in that caſe alſo reſembling in a ſort the Sonne of God, whoſe generation vvas extraordinarie, miraculous, and ineffable, according to both his natures, lacking a father in the one, and a mother in the other. his perſon hauing neither beginning nor ending, and his kingdom, and Prieſthod ſpecially, in him ſelf and in the Church, being eternall, both in reſpect of the time paſt, and the time to come: as the ſaid Doctor in the ſame epiſtle writeth. \fq ¹ \ft The resemblance of Melchisedec to Christ, in many points.\ef*
\v 4 And behold hovv great this man is, to vvhom alſo Abraham the Patriarke gaue tithes of the principal things. \ef - \fr 7.4 \fq Behold. \ft ¹ To proue that Chriſtes Prieſthod far paſſeth the Prieſthod of Aaron: and the Prieſthod of the new Teſtament, the Prieſthod of the old lavv: and conſequently that the ſacrifice of our Sauiour and the ſacrifice of the Church doth much excel the ſacrifices of Moyſes law, he diſputeth profoundly of the preeminences of Melchiſedec aboue the great Patriarch Abraham, who was father of the Leuites. \fq ¹ \ft By the sundrie excellencies of Melchisedecks Priesthod is proued the excellencie of the Priesthod & sacrifice of the new Testament.\ef* \ef - \fr 7.4 \fq Tithes. \ft The firſt preeminence, that Abraham paied tithes, and that of the beſt and moſt cheefe things that he had, vnto Melchiſedec, as a duety and homage, nor for him ſelf onely in perſon, but for Leui, who yet vvas not borne, and ſo for the vvhole Prieſthod of Leuies ſtocke, ¹acknovvledging thereby, Melchiſedec not onely to be a Prieſt, but his Prieſt and Superior, and ſo of al the Leuitical order. And it is here to be obſerued, that whereas in the 14 of Geneſis, vvhence this holy narration is taken, both in the Hebrue, and in the 70, it ſtandeth indifferent or doubtfull, vvhether Melchiſedec paied tithes to Abraham, or tooke tithes of him: the Apoſtle here putteth al out of controuerſie, plainely declaring that Abraham paied tithes to the other, as the inferiour to his Prieſt and Superior. And touching paiment of ² tithes, it is a natural duety, that men ovve to God in al lavves, and to be giuen to his Prieſts in his behalfe, for their honour and liuelihod. Iacob promiſed or vowed to pay them, \+it Gen. 28\+it*. Moyſes appointed them \+it Leuit. 27. Num. 18. Deut. 12. 14. 26\+it*. Chriſt confirmeth that duety \+it Mat. 23\+it*: and Abraham ſpecially here giueth them to Melchiſedec, plainely thereby approuing them or their equiualent to be due to Chriſt and the Prieſthod of the nevv Teſtament, much more then either in the lavv of Moyſes, or in the lavv of Nature. Of vvhich tithes due to the Clergie of Chriſtes Church ſee S. Cyprian \+it ep. 66\+it*. S. Hierom \+it ep. 1. c. 7\+it*. and \+it ep. 2. c. 5\+it*. to Heliodorus and Nepotianus. S. Auguſtine \+it ſer. 119 de tempore\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft He receiued tithes of Abraham, & consequently of Leui & Aaron. \fq ² \ft Tithes.\ef*
\v 5 And certes \x - \xt Nu. 18, 21. Deu. 18, 1. Ios. 14, 4.\x* they of the ſonnes of Leui that take the prieſthod, haue commaundement to take tithes of the people according to the Lavv, that is to ſay, of their brethren: albeit them ſelues alſo iſſued out of the loines of Abraham.
\v 6 but he whoſe generation is not numbered among them, tooke tithes of Abraham, and bleſſed him that had the promiſes.
\v 7 But without al contradiction, that vvhich is leſſe, is bleſſed of the better. \ef - \fr 7.7 \fq Is bleſſed of the better. \ft ¹ The ſecond preeminence, that Melchiſedec did bleſſe Abraham: vvhich vve ſee here S. Paul maketh a great and ſoueraine holy thing, grounding our Sauiours prerogatiue aboue the whole order of Aaron therein: and we ſee that in this ſort it is the proper act of Prieſthod: and that without al controuerſie as the Apoſtle ſaith, ² he is greater in dignitie, that hath authority to bleſſe, then the perſon that hath not, and therfore the Prieſts vocation to be in this behalfe far aboue any earthly king, vvho hath not power to giue benediction in this ſacred maner, neither to man, nor other creature. As here Melchiſedec, ſo Chriſt bleſſed much more, and ſo haue the Biſhops of his Church done, and do. Vvhich no man can maruel that our forefathers haue ſo highly eſteemed and ſought for, if he marke the vvonderful myſterie and grace thereof here expreſſed. This Patriarch alſo vvhich here taketh bleſſing of Melchiſedec, him ſelf (though in an inferior ſort) bleſſed his ſonnes, as the other Patriarches did, and fathers do their children by that example. \fq ¹ \ft He blessed Abraham. \fq ² \ft Blessing a great preeminence, specially in Priests.\ef*
\v 8 And here in deede, \f 1 \fr 7.8 \ft ∷ The tithes giuen to Melchiſedech vvere not giuen as to a mere mortal man, as al of the tribe of Leui & Aarons order were: but as to one repreſenting the Sonne of God, vvho now liueth and reigneth and holdeth his prieſthod & the functions therof for euer.\f* men that die, receiue tithes: but there he hath vvitnes, that he liueth.
\v 9 And (that it may ſo be ſaid) by Abraham Leui alſo, which receiued tithes, was tithed.
\v 10 for as yet he vvas in his fathers loines, vvhen Melchiſedec mette him.
\v 11 If then conſummation was by the Leuitical prieſthod (for vnder it the people receiued the Law) vvhat neceſſitie vvas there yet an other prieſt to riſe according to the order of Melchiſedec, and not to be called according to the order of Aaron? \ef - \fr 7.11 \fq If conſummation. \ft ¹ The principal propoſition of the whole epiſtle and al the Apoſtles diſcourſe, is inferred & grounded vpon the former prerogatiues of Melchiſedec aboue Abraham and Leui: that is, that the end, perfection, accompliſhment, and conſummation of al mans dueties and debtes to God, by the general redemption, ſatisfaction, full price and perfect ranſom of al mankind, vvas not achieued by any or al the Prieſts of Aarons order, nor by any ſacrifice or act of that Prieſthod, or of al the law of Moyſes, which vvas grounded vpon the Leuitical Prieſthod, but by Chriſt and his Prieſthod, vvhich is of the order and rite of Melchiſedec. \fq ¹ \ft The ful accomplishment of mans redemption was not by Aarons but by Melchisedecks Priesthod.\ef* \ef - \fr 7.11 \fq What neceſſitie. \ft ¹ This diſputation of the preeminence of Chriſtes Prieſthod aboue the Leuitical order, is againſt the erroneous perſuaſion of the Iewes, that thought their law, Prieſthod, and ſacrifices to be euerlaſting, and to be ſufficient in them ſelues, without any other Prieſt then Aaron and his ſucceſſors, and without al relation to Chriſtes Paſſion or any other redemption or remiſſion, then that vvhich their Leuitical offices did procure: not knovving that they vvere all figures of Chriſtes death, and to be ended and accompliſhed in the ſame. Which point vvell vnderſtood and kept in mind, vvill cleere the vvhole controuerſie betvvixt the Catholikes and Proteſtants, concerning the ſacrifice of the Church. for, the ſcope of the Apoſtles diſputation being, to auouch the dignity, preeminence, neceſſitie, and eternal fruite and effect of Chriſtes Paſſion, he had not to treate at all of the other, vvhich is a ſacrifice depending of his Paſſion, ſpecially writing to the Hebrues, that vvere to be inſtructed and reformed firſt touching the ſacrifice of the Croſſe, before they could fruitfully heare any thing of the other. though in couert and by moſt euident ſequele of diſputation, the learned and faithfull may eaſily perceiue vvherevpon the ſaid Sacrifice of the Church (vvhich is the Maſſe) is grounded. And therfore S. Hierom ſaith, \+it ep. 126\+it*: that al theſe commendations of Melchiſedec are in the type of Chriſt, \+it cuius profectus Eccleſiæ ſacramenta ſunt\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle to confute the Iewes false persuasion of Aarons Priesthod and sacrifices, speaketh altogether of the sacrifice of the Crosse.\ef*
\v 12 For the prieſthod being tranſlated, it is neceſſarie that a tranſlation of the Law alſo be made. \ef - \fr 7.12 \fq Tranſlated. \ft ¹ Note wel this place, and you ſhal perceiue thereby, that euery lavvful forme and manner of lavv, ſtate, or gouernement of Gods people dependeth on Prieſthod, riſeth, ſtandeth, falleth, or altereth with the Prieſthod. In the lavv of Nature, the ſtate of the people hanged on one kind of Prieſthod: in the lavv of Moyſes, of an other: in the ſtate of Chriſtianity, of an other: and therfore in the former ſentence the Apoſtle ſaid, that the Iewiſh people or Commonvvealth had their law vnder the Leuitical Prieſthod, and the Greeke ² more properly expreſſeth the matter, that they vvere \+it legitimated\+it*, that is to ſay, made a lawful people or communitie vnder God, by the Prieſthod. for there is no iuſt nor lavvful Commonvvealth in the world, that is not made legal and Gods peculiar, and diſtinguiſhed from vnlawful Commonweales that hold of falſe goddes, or of none at al, by Prieſthod. ³ Vvherevpon it is cleere, that the new lavv, and al Chriſtian peoples holding of the ſame, is made lavvful by the Prieſthod of the nevv Teſtament, and that the Proteſtants ſhamefully are deceiued, and deceiue others, that vvould haue Chriſtian Commonvveales to lacke an external Prieſthod, or Chriſtes death to aboliſh the ſame. for, this is a demonſtration, that if Chriſt haue aboliſhed Prieſthod, he hath aboliſhed the nevv law, which is the new Teſtament and ſtate of Grace, which al Chriſtian Commonwealths liue vnder. Neither vvere it true, that the Prieſthod vvere tranſlated with the Law, if al external Prieſthod ended by Chriſtes death, where the new lavv began. for ſo the lavv ſhould not depend on Prieſthod, but dure vvhen al Prieſthod vvere ended: which is againſt S. Paules doctrine. ⁴ Furthermore it is to be noted, that this legitimation or putting Communities vnder law, and Prieſthod, of what order ſoeuer, is no otherwiſe, but by ioyning one vvith an other in one homage of ſacrifice external, which is the proper act of Prieſthod. for, as no lavvful ſtate can be without prieſthod, ſo no prieſthod can be vvithout ſacrifice. And vve meane alwaies of Prieſthod & ſacrifice taken in their ovvne proper ſignification, as here S. Paul taketh them. for, the conſtitution difference, alteration, or tranſlation of ſtates and lavves riſe not vpon any mutation of ſpiritual or metaphorically taken Prieſthod, or ſacrifice: but vpon thoſe things in proper acception, as it is moſt plaine. Laſtly, it foloweth of this, that though Chriſt truely ſacrificed him ſelf vpon the Croſſe (there alſo a Prieſt according to the order of Melchiſedec) and there made the ful redemption of the world, confirmed, and conſummated his compact, and Teſtament, and the lavv and prieſthod of this his new and eternal ſtate, by his bloud: ⁵ yet that can not be the forme of ſacrifice into vvhich the old Prieſthod and ſacrifices vvere tranſlated, wherevpon the Apoſtle inferreth the tranſlation of the Law. For they all vvere figures of Chriſtes death, and ended in effect at his death, yet they vvere not altered into that kind of ſacrifice, vvhich was to be made but once, and vvas executed in ſuch a ſort, that peoples and nations Chriſtened could not meete often to worſhip at it, nor haue their law and Prieſtes conſtituted in the ſame. though for the honour and duety, remembrance and repreſentation thereof, not onely vve Chriſtians, but alſo al peoples faithful both of Iewes & Gentiles, haue had their prieſthod and ſacrifices according to the difference of their ſtates. Which kind of Sacrifices vvere tranſlated one into an other: and ſo no doubt is the Prieſthod Leuitical properly turned into the Prieſthod and ſacrifice of the Church, according to Melchiſedecks rite, and Chriſtes inſtitution in the formes of bread and vvine. See the next note. \fq ¹ \ft No lawful state of people without an external Priesthod. \fq ² \ft \+it νενομοθέτητο\+it* \fq ³ \ft External Priesthod necessarie for the state of the new Testament. \fq ⁴ \ft External sacrifice also necessarie for the same. \fq ⁵ \ft The translation of the old Priesthod & sacrifices, must needes be into the said Priesthod and sacrifice of the Church.\ef*
\v 13 For he on vvhom theſe things be ſaid, is of an other tribe, of the vvhich, none attended on the altar.
\v 14 For it is manifeſt that our Lord ſprung of Iuda: in the which tribe Moyſes ſpake nothing of \f 1 \fr 7.14 \fq `prieſtes \ft \+it Prieſthod.\+it*\f* `prieſtes'.
\v 15 And yet it is much more euident: if according to the ſimilitude of Melchiſedec there ariſe an other prieſt,
\v 16 vvhich vvas not made according to the Lavv of the carnal commaundement, but according to the power of life indiſſoluble.
\v 17 For he witneſſeth, \x - \xt Ps. 109, 4.\x* \it That thou art a prieſt for euer, according to the order of Melchiſedec\it*. \ef - \fr 7.17 \fq A Prieſt for euer. \ft ¹ Chriſt is not called a Prieſt for euer, only for that his perſon is eternal, or for that he ſitteth on the right hand of God, and perpetually praieth or maketh interceſsion for vs, or for that the effect of his death is euerlaſting: for all this proueth not that in proper ſignification his Prieſthod is perpetual: but according to the iudgement of all the fathers grounded vpon this deepe and diuine diſcourſe of S. Paul, and vpon the very nature, definition, and propriety of Prieſthod, and the excellent act and order of Melchiſedech, and the ſtate of the new law, he is a Prieſt for euer according to Melchiſedech's order, ² ſpecially in reſpect of the ſacrifice of his holy body and blood, inſtituted at his laſt ſupper, and executed by his commiſsion, commandment, and perpetual concurrence with his Prieſts, in the forms of bread and wine: in vvhich things only the ſaid high Prieſt Melchiſedech did ſacrifice. For though S. Paul make no expreſs mention hereof, becauſe of the depth of the myſterie, and their incredulity or feebleneſſe to vvhom he wrote: yet it is euident in the iudgement of all the learned Fathers (vvithout exception) that euer wrote either vpon this epiſtle, or vpon the 14. of Geneſis, or the Pſalm 109, or by occaſion haue treated of the ſacrifice of the altar, that the eternity and proper act of Chriſt's Prieſthod, and conſequently the immutability of the new law, conſiſteth in the perpetual offering of Chriſt's body and blood in the Church. ³ Which thing is ſo well known to the Aduerſaries of Chriſt's Church and Prieſthod, and ſo granted, that they be forced impudently to cauill vpon certaine Hebrew particles, that Melchiſedech did not offer in bread and vvine: yea and vvhen that will not ſerue, plainly to deny him to haue been a Prieſt: vvhich is to giue checkmate to the Apoſtle, and to ouerthrow all his diſcourſe. Thus whiles theſe vvicked men pretend to defend Chriſt's only Prieſthod, they indeed aboliſh as much as in them lieth, the whole order, office, and ſtate of his eternall law and Prieſthod. ⁴ Arnobius ſaith, \+it By the myſterie of bread and vvine he was made a Prieſt for euer\+it*. And againe, \+it The eternal memory, by which he gaue the food of his body to them that feare him. in Pſal. 109. 110.\+it* Lactantius, \+it In the Church he muſt needs haue his eternal Prieſthod according to the Order of Melchiſedech. Li. 14. inſtitut.\+it* S. Hierom to Euagrius ⁵, \+it Aaron's Prieſthod had an end, but Melchiſedech's, that is Chriſt's and the Church's is perpetuall,\+it* ⁶ \+it both for the time paſt and to come.\+it* S. Chryſoſtom therefore calleth the Church's Sacrifice, \+it hoſtiam inconſumptibilem, an hoſt or Sacrifice that can not be conſumed. ho. 17. in 9. Hebr.\+it* S. Cyprian, \+it hoſtiam qua ſublata, nulla eſſet futura religio,\+it* an hoſt vvhich being taken away, there could be no religion. \+it de Coena Domini. nu 2.\+it* Emiſſenus, \+it perpetuam oblationem & perpetuo currentem redemptionem, a perpetual oblation and a redemption that runneth or continueth euerlaſtingly. ho. 5. de Paſch.\+it* And our Sauiour expreſſeth ſo much in the very inſtitution of the B. Sacrament of his body and blood: ſpecially vvhen he calleth the later kind, \+it the new Teſtament in his blood,\+it* ſignifying that as the old law vvas eſtabliſhed in the blood of beaſts, ſo the new (vvhich is his eternal Teſtament) ſhould be dedicated and perpetual in his owne blood: not only as it vvas ſhed on the Croſs, but as giuen in the Chalice. And therefore into this ſacrifice of the altar (ſaith S. Auguſtine \+it li. 17. de Civit. c. 20.\+it* S. Leo \+it ſer. 8. de Paſsione\+it*, and the reſt) vvere the old ſacrifices to be tranſlated. See S. Cyprian \+it ep. 63. ad Cæcil. nu. 2.\+it* S. Ambroſe \+it de Sacram. li. 5. c. 4.\+it* S. Auguſtine \+it in Pſal. 33. Conc. 2.\+it* and \+it li. 17. de Civit. c. 17.\+it* S. Hierom \+it ep. 17. c. 2. & ep. 126.\+it* Epiph. \+it hær. 55.\+it* Theodoret \+it in Pſal. 109.\+it* Damaſcene \+it li. 4. c. 14.\+it* Finally if any of the Fathers, or all the Fathers, had either wiſdom, grace, or intelligence of God's worde and myſteries, this is the truth. If nothing will ſerue our Aduerſaries, Chriſt Jeſus confound them, and defend his eternal Prieſthod, and ſtate of his new Teſtament eſtabliſhed in the ſame. \fq ¹ \ft How Christ is a Priest for euer. \fq ² \ft Christs eternal Priesthod consisteth in the perpetual sacrifice of his body & bloud in the Church. \fq ³ \ft The Protestants cauilling vpon particles, against Melchisedecks sacrifice & Priesthod, directly against the Apostle. \fq ⁴ \ft Christs eternal Priesthod and sacrifice in the Church is proued out of the fathers. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Ep. 126\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft * \+it That is, from Adam to the end of the world, represented by sacrifice\+it*.\ef*
\v 18 Reprobation certes is made of the former commaundement, becauſe of the weakeneſſe and vnprofitableneſſe thereof. \ef - \fr 7.18 \fq Of the former commandment. \ft ¹ The whole law of Moyſes containing all their old Prieſthood, Sacrifice, Sacraments and ceremonies is called the \+it Old commandment\+it*: and the new Teſtament containing the Sacrifice of Chriſt's body and blood, and all the Sacraments and graces given by the ſame, is named the \+it New mandatum\+it*: for which our forefathers called the Thurſday in the Holy Week, ² \+it Maundy Thurſday\+it*, becauſe that in it, the new law and Teſtament was dedicated in the Chalice of his blood: the old \+it mandatum\+it*, law, Prieſthood, and ſacrifices, for that they were inſufficient and unperfect, being taken away: and this new ſacrifice, after the order of Melchiſedech, given in the place thereof. \fq ¹ \ft The old commandment, and the new. \fq ² \ft Maundy Thursday why so called.\ef*
\v 19 For the Lavv brought nothing to perfection, but an introduction of a better hope, by the vvhich we approche to God. \ef - \fr 7.19 \fq The introduction. \ft ¹ Ever obſerve, that the abrogation of the old law, is not an aboliſhing of all Prieſthood, but an introduction of a new, containing the hope of eternal things, where the old had but temporal. \fq ¹ \ft The introduction of a new Priesthood.\ef*
\v 20 And in as much as it is not vvithout an othe, (the other truely vvithout an othe vvere made prieſtes:
\v 21 but this vvith an othe, by him that ſaid vnto him: \x - \xt Ps. 109, 4.\x* \it Our Lord hath ſvvorne, and it ſhal not repent him: thou art a prieſt for euer\it*) \ef - \fr 7.21 \fq With an oath. \ft ¹ This oath ſignifieth the infallible and abſolute promiſe of the eternity of the new Prieſthood and ſtate of the Church: Chriſt by his death, and blood ſhed in the ſacrifice of the Croſs, confirming it, ſealing it, and making himſelf the ſurety and pledge thereof. For though the new Teſtament was inſtituted, given, and dedicated in the Supper, yet the warrant, confirmation, and eternal operation thereof, was achieved upon the Croſs, in the one oblation and one general and everlaſting redemption there made. \fq ¹ \ft The eternity of the new Priesthood confirmed by the Fathers oath, & Christ's passion.\ef*
\v 22 by ſo much, is \sc IESVS\sc* made a ſuretie of a better teſtament.
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 7.23 \ft The Epiſtle upon S. Leo his day Iunii 28. and for ſome other Confeſſors Biſhops.\f* And the other in deede vvere made prieſtes, being many, becauſe that by death they vvere prohibited to continue: \ef - \fr 7.23 \fq Being many. \ft ¹ The Proteſtants not underſtanding this place, feign very fooliſhly, that the Apoſtle ſhould make this difference betwixt the old ſtate and the new: that in the old, there were many Prieſts: in the new, none at all but Chriſt. Which is againſt the Prophet Eſay, ſpecially propheſying of the Prieſts of the new Teſtament (as S. Hierom declareth upon the ſame place) in theſe words, ² \+it You ſhall be called the\+it* ³ \+it Prieſts of God: the\+it* ⁴ \+it miniſters of our God, ſhall it be ſaid to you:\+it* & it taketh away all viſible Prieſthood, & conſequently the lawful ſtate that the Church and God's people have in earth, with all Sacraments and external worſhip. ⁵ The Apoſtle then meaneth firſt, that the abſolute ſacrifice of conſummation, perfection, and univerſal redemption, was but one, once done, and by one only Prieſt done, and therefore it could not be any of the ſacrifices, or all the ſacrifices of the Jews' law, or wrought by any or by all of them, becauſe they were a number at once, and ſucceeding one another, every of their offices and functions ending by their death, and could not work ſuch an eternal redemption as by Chriſt only was wrought upon the Croſs. Secondly, S. Paul inſinuateth thereupon, that Chriſt never loſeth the dignity or practice of his eternal Prieſthood, by death nor otherwiſe, never yieldeth it up to any, never hath ſucceſſors after him, that may enter into his room or right of Prieſthood, as Aaron and all other had in the Levitical Prieſthood, but that himſelf worketh and concurreth with his miniſters the Prieſts of the new Teſtament, in all their acts of Prieſthood, as well of ſacrifice as Sacrament, bleſſing, preaching, praying, and the like whatſoever. This therefore was the fault of the Hebrews, that they did not acknowledge their Levitical ſacrifices and Prieſthood to be reformed and perfited by Chriſt's ſacrifice on the Croſs: and againſt them the Apoſtle only diſputeth, and not againſt our Prieſts of holy Church, or the number of them, who all confeſs their Prieſthood and all exerciſes of the ſame, to depend upon Chriſt's only perpetual Prieſthood. \fq ¹ \ft By the comparison of many Priests, & one, is not meant that there is but one Priest of the new Testament. \fq ² \ft \+it Esa. c. 61.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * ἱερεῖς.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * λειτουργοὶ.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The meaning is, that the absolute sacrifice of eternal redemption could not be done by those many Aaronical Priests but by one only, Christ Jesus: who liveth a Priest for ever, hath no successor, and as chief Priest, worketh and concurreth with all Priests in their Priestly functions.\ef*
\v 24 but this, for that he continueth for euer, hath an euerlaſting prieſthod.
\v 25 vvhereby he is able to ſaue alſo for euer \f 1 \fr 7.25 \fq `going \ft `them that goe\f* `going' by him ſelf to God: \f 2 \fr 7.25 \ft ∷ Chriſt according to his humane nature praieth for vs, & continually repreſenteth his former paſſion and merites to God the Father.\f* alvvaies liuing to make interceſſion for vs.
\v 26 For it vvas ſeemely that vve ſhould haue ſuch a high prieſt, holy, innocent, impolluted, ſeparated from ſinners, and made higher then the heauens.
\v 27 vvhich hath not neceſſitie daily (as the prieſtes) firſt \x - \xt Lev. 9, 7. 16, 6.\x* for his ovvne ſinnes to offer hoſtes, then for the peoples. for this he did once, in offering him ſelf. ⊢ \ef - \fr 7.27 \fq This did he once. \ft This is the ſpecial preeminence of Chriſt, that he offereth for other men's ſins only, having none of his own to offer for, as all other Prieſts both of the old and new law have. And this again is the ſpecial dignity of his own perſon, not communicable to any other of what order or Prieſthood ſoever, that he by his death (which is the only oblation that is by the Apoſtle declared to be irreiterable in itſelf) payed the one full ſufficient ranſom for the redemption of all ſins.\ef*
\v 28 For the Law appointeth prieſtes them that haue infirmitie: but the vvord of the othe vvhich is after the Lavv, the Sonne for euer perfected.
\c 8
\cd Out of the ſame Pſalme 109 he vrgeth this alſo, Sit thou on my right hand, ſhewing that the Leuitical tabernacle on earth, was but a ſhadow of his true Tabernacle in heauen: without which he ſhould not be a Prieſt at all: 6 Whereas he is of a better Prieſthod then they, as alſo he proueth by the excellencie of the new Teſtament aboue the old.
\p
\v 1 \sc BVT\sc* the ſumme concerning thoſe things vvhich be ſaid, is: VVe haue ſuch an high prieſt, vvho is ſette on the right hand of the ſeate of maieſtie in the heauens,
\v 2 a \f 1 \fr 8.2 \ft Chriſt liuing and reigning in heauen, continueth his prieſtly function ſtil, and is miniſter not of Moyſes Sancta & tabernacle, but of his owne body & bloud, which be the true holies, and tabernacle, not formed by man, but by Gods owne hand.\f* miniſter of the holies, and of the true tabernacle, vvhich our Lord pight & not man.
\v 3 For euery high prieſt is appointed to offer giftes and hoſtes: vvherfore it is neceſſarie that he alſo haue ſome thing that he may offer: \ef - \fr 8.3 \fq Neceſſarie that he alſo. \ft ¹ Euen now being in heauen, becauſe he is a Biſhop and Prieſt, he muſt needes haue ſomewhat to offer, and vvherein to do ſacrifice: and that not in ſpiritual ſort onely, for that could not make him a Prieſt of any certaine order. And it is moſt falſe and vvicked, to hold vvith the Caluiniſtes, ² that Melchiſedecks Prieſthod vvas vvholy ſpiritual. For then Chriſts death vvas not a corporal, external, viſible, and truely named ſacrifice: neither could Chriſt or Melchiſedec be any otherwiſe a Prieſt, then euery faithful man is: vvhich to hold (as the Caluiniſts folowing their owne doctrine muſt needes do) is directly againſt the Scriptures, and no leſſe againſt Chriſtes one oblation of his body vpon the Croſſe, then it is againſt the daily ſacrifice of his body vpon the altar. Therfore he hath a certaine hoſt in external and proper maner, to make perpetual oblation thereby in the Church: for, viſible and external act of ſacrificing in heauen he doth not exerciſe. \fq ¹ \ft Christs priesthod & sacrifice is external, not spiritual only. \fq ² \ft * \+it Beza in schol. Test. Græcolat. in c.7 Heb. num. 8\+it*.\ef*
\v 4 if then he vvere vpon the earth, neither vvere he a prieſt: vvhereas there vvere that did offer giftes according to the Lavv, \ef - \fr 8.4 \fq If vpon the earth. \ft ¹ It is by his death, and reſurrection to life againe, that his body is become apt and fitte in ſuch diuine ſort to be ſacrificed perpetually. For if he had liued in mortal ſort ſtill, that vvay of myſtical repreſentation of breaking his body and ſeparating the bloud from the ſame, could not haue been agreable. and ſo the Church and Chriſtian people ſhould haue lacked a prieſthod and ſacrifice, & Chriſt him ſelf ſhould not haue been a Prieſt of a peculiar order, but either muſt haue offered in the things that Aarons Prieſts did, or els haue been no Prieſt at all. For, to haue offered onely ſpiritually, as all faithful men do, that could not be ynough for his vocation, and our redemption, and ſtate of the new Teſtament. How his fleſh was made fit to be offered and eaten in the B. Sacrament, by his death, ſee Iſychius \+it li. 1 in leuit. cap. 2\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft How Christes body is made fit to be sacrificed and eaten perpetually.\ef*
\v 5 that \x - \xt Exo. 25, 9. 40.\x* ſerue the exampler & ſhadow of heauenly things. As it vvas anſvvered Moyſes, vvhen he finiſhed the tabernacle, \f 2 \fr 8.5 \ft c λατρεύουσι\f* See (quod he) that thou make al things according to the exampler vvhich vvas ſhevved thee in the mount. \ef - \fr 8.5 \fq Heauenly things. \ft ¹ As the Church or ſtate of the new Teſtament is commonly called \+it Regnum cælorum & Dei\+it*, in the Scriptures, ſo theſe heauenly things be probably taken by learned men, for the myſteries of the new Teſtament. And it ſeemeth that the paterne giuen to Moyſes to frame his tabernacle by, was the Church, rather then the heauens them ſelues: al S. Paules diſcourſe tending to ſhew the difference betwixt the new Teſtament and the old, and not to make compariſon betwene the ſtate of heauen and the old law. Though incidently, becauſe the condition of the new Teſtament more neerely reſembleth the ſame, then the old ſtate doth, he ſometime may ſpeake ſomewhat therof alſo. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Kingdom of heauen, and heauenly things\+it*, spoke of the Church.\ef*
\v 6 But novv he hath obtained a better miniſterie, by ſo much as he is mediatour of a better teſtament, vvhich is eſtabliſhed in better promiſes.
\v 7 For \f 1 \fr 8.7 \ft The promiſes and effectes of the Law were temporal, but the promiſes and effectes of Chriſtes Sacraments in the Church be eternal.\f* if that former had been void of fault, there ſhould not certes a place of a ſecōd been ſought.
\v 8 For blaming them, he ſaith: \x - \xt Hier. 31, 31.\x* \it Behold the daies ſhal come, ſaith our Lord: and I wil conſummate vpon the houſe of Iſrael, and vpon the houſe of Iuda a new Teſtament\it*:
\v 9 \it not according to the teſtament vvhich I made to their fathers in the day that I tooke their hand to bring them\it* out of the land of Ægypt: becauſe they did not continue \it in my teſtament: and I neglected them, ſaith our Lord\it*.
\v 10 \it For this is the teſtament vvhich I vvil diſpoſe to the houſe of Iſrael after thoſe daies, ſaith our Lord: Giuing my Lavves into their minde, & in their hart vvil I ſuperſcribe them: and I vvil be their God, and they ſhal be my people\it*: \ef - \fr 8.10 \fq Into their minde. \ft ¹ This alſo and the reſt folowing is fulfilled in the Church, and is the proper effect of the new Teſtament, which is the grace and ſpirit of loue, graffed in the hartes of the faithful by the holy Ghoſt, vvorking in the Sacraments and ſacrifice of the new law to that effecte. \fq ¹ \ft Grace, the effect of the new Testament.\ef* \ef - \fr 8.10 \fq Their God. \ft ¹ This mutual couenant made betwixt God and the faithful, is that vvhich vvas dedicated and eſtabliſhed, firſt in the chalice of his bloud, called therfore ² \+it the new Teſtament in his bloud\+it*: and vvhich vvas ſtraight after ratified by the death of the teſtator, vpon the Croſſe. \fq ¹ \ft The new Testament or couenant betwene God & man. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 22\+it*.\ef*
\v 11 \it and euery one ſhall not teach his neighbour, and euery one his brother, ſaying, Knovv our Lord: becauſe al ſhal know me from the leſſer to the greater of them\it*: \ef - \fr 8.11 \fq Shal not teach. \ft So it was in the primitiue Church, in ſuch ſpecially as were the firſt founders of our new ſtate in Chriſt. And that vvhich vvas verified in the Apoſtles and other principal men, the Apoſtle ſpeaketh generally as though it vvere ſo in the vvhole. as S. Peter ¹ applieth the like out of Ioël, and our Sauiour ² ſo ſpeaketh, vvhen he ſaith that ſuch as beleeue in him, ſhal vvorke miracles of diuers ſortes. ³ Chriſtian men then muſt not abuſe this place to make chalenge of new inſpirations and ſo great knowledge that they neede no Scriptures or teaching in this life, as ſome Heretikes doe: vvith much like reaſon and ſhew of Scriptures as the Proteſtants haue to refuſe external ſacrifice. And it is no leſſe phantaſtical madneſſe to deny external ſacrifice, ſacraments, or Prieſthod, then it is to aboliſh teaching and preaching. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Act. 2\+it*. \fq ² \ft \+it Io. 14. v. 12\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Scriptures abused for phantastical inspirations.\ef*
\v 12 \it becauſe I wil be merciful to their iniquities, & their ſinnes I wil not now remember\it*.
\v 13 And in ſaying a nevv, the former he hath made old. And that vvhich grovveth auncient and vvaxeth old, is nigh to vtter decay.
\c 9
\cd In the old Teſtament, that ſecular Sanctuarie had two partes: the one ſignifying that time, with the ceremonies therof for the emundation of the fleſh: the other ſignifying heauen, which then vvas ſhut, vntil our High prieſt Chriſt entered into it, and that with his owne bloud, ſhed for the emundation of our conſciences. Wherevpon he concludeth the excellencie of his tabernacle and hoſt aboue the old. 25 Noting alſo the difference, that he entered but once (ſo effectual was that one blouddy offering of him ſelf, for euer) wheras the Leuitical High prieſt entered euery yere once.
\p
\v 1 \sc THE\sc* \x - \xt Exo. 25, 26. 1, 36.\x*\f 2 \fr 9.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Imber Saturday \+it in Sept.\+it*\f* former alſo in deede had iuſtifications \f 3 \fr 9.1 \ft \+it c λατρείας.\+it*\f* of ſeruice, and a ſecular ſanctuarie.
\v 2 For the tabernacle vvas made, the firſt, wherein vvere, the candleſtickes, and the table, and the propoſition of loaues, vvhich is called Holy.
\v 3 But after the ſecond vele, the tabernacle, vvhich is called \it Sancta Sanctorum\it*:
\v 4 hauing a golden cenſar, and the arke of the teſtament couered about on euery part vvith gold, in the vvhich vvas ″ a golden potte hauing Manna, and the rod of Aaron that had bloſſomed, & \x - \xt 3. Reg. 8. 2. Par. 5.\x* the tables of the teſtament, \ef - \fr 9.4 \fq A golden potte. \ft ¹ The Proteſtants count it ſuperſtitious to keepe vvith honour and reuerence the holy memories or monuments of Gods benefites and miracles, or the tokens of Chriſtes Paſſion, as his Croſſe, garments, or other things appertaining to him or his Saincts, and thinke it impoſſible that ſuch things ſhould dure ſo long: vvhen they may here ſee the reuerent and long reſeruation of Manna, ² which of it ſelf vvas moſt apt to putrifie, and of Aarons rodde, onely for that it ſodenly floriſhed by miracle, the tables of the Teſtament &c. See a notable place in S. Cyril \+it li. 6 cont. Iulian\+it*. where he defendeth againſt Iulian the Apoſtataes blaſphemie, the keeping and honouring of that ³ Croſſe or vvood which Chriſt died on. See alſo S. Paulinus \+it ep. 11\+it*. and what reuerence S. Hierom and the faithful of his time did to the ⁴ ſepulchres of Chriſt and his Martyrs, and to their relikes. \+it We reuerence and worſhip\+it* (ſaith he) ⁵ \+it euery where Martyrs ſepulchres, and putting the holy aſhes to our eies, if we may, vve touch it with our mouth alſo: and do ſome thinke, that the monument wherein our Lord was buried, is to be neglected\+it*? But our Proteſtants can not ſkill of this. they had rather folovv Vigilantius, Iulianus the Apoſtata, and ſuch Maiſters, then the holy Doctors and euident practiſe of the Church in al ages. \fq ¹ \ft Relikes. \fq ² \ft They continue without putrefaction. \fq ³ \ft The holy CROSSE. \fq ⁴ \ft The sepulchres of Christ and his Saincts. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it ep. 17. c. 5\+it*.\ef*
\v 5 and ouer it vvere \x - \xt Exo. 25, 22.\x* the ″ Cherubins of glorie ouerſhadovving the propitiatorie. of vvhich things it is not needeful to ſpeake novv particularly. \ef - \fr 9.5 \fq Cherubins. \ft You ſee it is a fond thing, to conclude vpon the firſt or ſecond commaundement, that there ſhould be no ſacred images in the Church, when euen among theſe people that were moſt prone to idolatrie, and groſſe in imagination of ſpiritual things ſuch as Angels are, and to whom the precept was ſpecially giuen, the ſame God that forbade them grauen idols, ¹ did commaund theſe images of Angels to be made and ſet in the ſoueraine holieſt place of al the Tabernacle or Temple. By which it is plaine, that much more the images of Chriſt and his B. mother and Saincts, that may be more truely pourtered then mere ſpiritual ſubſtances can be, are not contrarie to Gods commaundement, nor againſt his honour, or repugnant to any other Scripture at all, which condemne onely the Idols or pourtraitures of the Heathen made for adoration of falſe Gods. \fq ¹ \ft Images in Salomons temple commaunded by God.\ef*
\v 6 But theſe things being ſo ordered, in the firſt tabernacle in deede the prieſts alvvaies entered, accompliſhing offices of the ſacrifices.
\v 7 But in the ſecond, \x - \xt Exo. 30, 10. Lev. 16, 2. 30.\x* once a yere the high prieſt only: not vvithout bloud vvhich he offereth for his ovvne and the peoples ignorance:
\v 8 the holy Ghoſt ſignifying this, that the vvay of the holies was \f 1 \fr 9.8 \ft The vvay to heauen was not open before Chriſts paſſion. & therfore the Patriarches and good men of the old Teſtament were in ſome other place of reſt vntil then.\f* not yet manifeſted, the former tabernacle as yet ſtanding.
\v 9 which is a cparable of the time preſent: according to vvhich are offered giftes and hoſtes, vvhich can not concerning the conſcience make perfect \f 1 \fr 9.9 \ft Al things done in the old Teſtament and prieſthod vvere figures of Chriſtes actions.\f*\f 2 \fr 9.9 \ft τὸν λατρεύοντα.\f* him that ſerueth,
\v 10 onely in meates and in drinkes, and diuerſe baptiſmes, and iuſtices of the fleſh laid on them ″ vntil the time of correction. \ef - \fr 9.10 \fq Vntil the time of correction. \ft Al thoſe groſſe and carnal ſacrifices, ceremonies, and obſeruations inſtituted to cleanſe and purifie the fleſh from legal irregularities and impurities onely, and not reaching to the purging of the ſoules & conſciences of men, being commaunded not for euer, but till Chriſtes comming, ceaſed then: and better, more forcible, and more ſpiritual Sacraments were inſtituted in their place. ¹ For vve may not imagine Chriſt to haue taken avvay the old, and put none in their place: or to alter the ſacraments onely into other ſacraments external, and not alſo to tranſlate the ſacrifices to ſome other more excellent. for it is called, \+it tempus correctionis, non abolitionis ſacrificij aut legis: the time of correction not of aboliſhing ſacrifice or lavv\+it*. Neither haue they more reaſon to affirme Chriſtes one oblation vpon the Croſſe to haue rather taken avvay al kind of ſacrifice, then al manner of Sacraments. The time and ſtate of the new Teſtament is not made lawleſſe, hoſtleſſe, or vvithout ſacrifice, but it is the time of correction or reformation and abettering al the foreſaid things. \fq ¹ \ft Sacrifice not taken away by the new Testament, but changed into a better.\ef*
\v 11 But \f 1 \fr 9.11 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Paſſion Sunday.\f* Chriſt aſſiſting an high Prieſt of the good things to come, by a more ample and more perfect tabernacle not made vvith hand, that is, not of this creation:
\v 12 neither by the bloud of goates or of calues, but by his ovvne bloud entered in once into the Holies, ″ eternal redemption being found. ⊢ \ef - \fr 9.12 \fq Eternal redemption. \ft ¹ No one of the ſacrifices, nor al the ſacrifices of the old law, could make that one general price, ranſom, and redemption of all mankind, and of al ſinnes, ſauing this one higheſt Prieſt Chriſt, and the one ſacrifice of his bloud once offered vpon the Croſſe. Vvhich ſacrifice of redemption can not be often done, becauſe Chriſt could not die but once. though the figures alſo thereof in the law of nature and of Moyſes, were truely called ſacrifices, as ſpecially this high and maruelous commemoration of the ſame in the holy Sacrament of the altar, according to the rite of the nevv Teſtament, is moſt truely and ſingularly (as S. Auguſtine ² calleth it) a ſacrifice. But neither this ſort, nor the other of the old lavv, being often repeated and done by many Prieſts (al which vvere and are ſinners them ſelues) could be the general redeeming and conſummating ſacrifice: nor any one of thoſe Prieſts, nor al the Prieſts together, either of the law of Nature, or of Aarons, or Melchiſedecks order (except Chriſt alone) coulde be the general redeemers of the world. ³ And this is the Apoſtles meaning in al this compariſon and oppoſition of Chriſtes death to the old ſacrifices, and of Chriſt to their Prieſts: and not that Chriſtes death or ſacrifice of the Croſſe ſhould take away al ſacrifices, or proue that thoſe Aaronical offices were no true ſacrifices at al, nor thoſe Prieſts, verily Prieſts. They vvere true Prieſts & true ſacrifices, though none of thoſe ſacrifices vvere the high, capital, and general ſacrifice of our price and redemption: nor none of them, or of thoſe Prieſts, could vvithout reſpect to this one ſacrifice of Chriſtes death, vvorke any thing to Gods honour, or remiſſion of ſinnes, as the Iewes did falſely imagine, not referring them at al to this general redemption and remiſsion by Chriſt, but thinking them to be abſolute ſacrifices in them ſelues. And that to haue been the errour of the Hebrues, you may read in S. Auguſtine \+it li. 3. doct. Chriſt. c. 6\+it*. And this, vve tel the Proteſtants, is the onely purpoſe of the Apoſtle. But they be ſo groſſe, or ignorant in the Scriptures, and ſo malitiouſly ſet againſt Gods and the Churches truth, that they peruerſely and foliſhly turne the whole diſputation againſt the ſacrifice of the B. Maſſe, and the Prieſts of the new Teſtament: as though we held, that the ſacrifice of the altar were the general redemption or redeeming ſacrifice, or that it had no relation to Chriſtes death, or that it were not the repreſentation and moſt liuely reſemblance of the ſame, or were not inſtituted and done, to apply in particular to the vſe of the partakers, that other general benefite of Chriſtes one oblation vpon the Croſſe. Againſt the Iewes then onely S. Paul diſputeth, and againſt the falſe opinion they had of their Prieſts and ſacrifices, to vvhich they attributed al remiſſion and redemption, without reſpect of Chriſtes death. \fq ¹ \ft One only sacrifice on the Crosse the redemption of the world: and one onely Priest (Christ) the redeemer thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. de Sp. & lit. c. 11\+it*. \fq ³ \ft The Apostles disputation being only against the errour of the Iewes concerning their sacrifices and priests: the Protestants applying it against the sacrifice of the Masse & priestes of the new Testament.\ef*
\v 13 For \x - \xt Lev. 9, 8. 16, 6. 14.\x* if the bloud of goates and of oxen & the aſhes of an heifer being ſprinkled, ſanctifieth the polluted to the cleanſing of the fleſh:
\v 14 hovv much more \f 1 \fr 9.14 \fq `hath \ft \+it ſhal\+it*\f* `hath' the bloud of Chriſt vvho by the holy Ghoſt offered him ſelf vnſpotted vnto God, \f 2 \fr 9.14 \fq `cleanſed \ft \+it cleanſe\+it*\f* `cleanſed' our conſcience from dead vvorkes, to ſerue the liuing God?
\v 15 And therfore he is the mediatour of the nevv Teſtament: that death being a meane, vnto the redemption ″ of theſe preuarications vvhich vvere vnder the former teſtament, they that are called may receiue the promiſe of eternal inheritance. ⊢ \ef - \fr 9.15 \fq Of thoſe preuarications. \ft The Proteſtants do vnlearnedly imagine, that becauſe al ſinnes be remitted by the force of Chriſtes paſſion, that therfore there ſhould be no other ſacrifice after his death. Vvhereas in deede they might as well ſay, there ought neuer to haue been ſacrifice appointed by God, either in the law of Nature, or of Moyſes: as al their arguments made againſt the Sacrifice of the Church vpon the Apoſtles diſcourſe, proue as wel, or rather onely, that there were no ſacrifices of Aarons order or Leuitical law at all. For againſt the Iewes falſe opinion concerning them, doth he diſpute, and not a word touching the ſacrifice of the Church, vnto which in al this diſcourſe he neuer oppoſeth Chriſtes ſacrifice vpon the Croſſe: al Chriſtian men vvel knovving that the hoſt & oblation of thoſe tvvo, though they differ in maner and external forme, yet is in deede al one. The Apoſtle then ſhevveth here plainely, that al the ſinnes that euer vvere remitted ſince the beginning of the vvorld, vvere no otherwiſe forgiuen, but by the force and in reſpect of Chriſtes Paſſion. Yet it followeth not thereupon, that the oblations of Abel, Abraham, Aaron, &c vvere no ſacrifices, as by the Heretikes fooliſh deduction it ſhould do: S. Paul not oppoſing Chriſtes Paſſion to them, for the intent to proue them to haue been no ſacrifices, but to proue, that they vvere not abſolute ſacrifices, nor the redeeming or conſummating Sacrifice, vvhich could not be many, nor done by many Prieſts, but by one, and at one time, by a more excellent Prieſt then any of them, or any other mere mortal man. ¹ And that you may ſee the blaſphemous pride and ignorance of Caluin, and in him, of al his fellovves: read (ſo many as may read Heretical bookes) his commentarie vpon this place, and there you ſhal ſee him gather vpon this, that Chriſtes death had force from the beginning & vvas the remedie for al ſinnes ſince the creation of the vvorld, therfore there muſt be no moe but that one ſacrifice of Chriſtes death. Vvhich muſt needs by his deduction hold (as it doth in deede) no leſſe againſt the old ſacrifices then the new ſacrifice of the Church, and ſo take avvay al, vvhich is againſt the Apoſtles meaning and al religion. \fq ¹ \ft Caluins argument against the sacrifice of the altar, maketh no lesse against the sacrifices of the old Law.\ef*
\v 16 For \x - \xt Gal. 3, 15.\x* vvhere there is a teſtament: the death of the teſtatour muſt of neceſſitie come betvvene.
\v 17 For a teſtament is confirmed in the dead: othervviſe it is yet of no value, vvhiles he that teſted, liueth.
\v 18 Vvherevpon neither vvas the firſt certes dedicated vvithout bloud.
\v 19 For al the commaundement of the Lavv being read of Moyſes to al the people: he taking the bloud of calues and goates vvith \f 1 \fr 9.19 \ft Here we may learne that the Scriptures conteine not al neceſſarie rites or truthes, when neither the place to which the Apoſtle alludeth, nor any other, mentioneth half theſe ceremonies, but he had them by tradition.\f* vvater and ſcarlet vvool and hyſſope, ſprinkled the very booke alſo it ſelf and al the people,
\v 20 ſaying, ″ \x - \xt Exo. 24, 8.\x* This is the bloud of the Teſtament, vvhich God hath commaunded vnto you. \ef - \fr 9.20 \fq This is the bloud. \ft ¹ Chriſtes death vvas neceſſarie for the full confirmation, ratification, and accompliſhement of the new Teſtament, though it vvas begonne to be dedicated in the ſacrifice of his laſt ſupper, being alſo within the compaſſe of his Paſſion. Which is euident by the vvordes pronounced by Chriſt ouer the holy chalice, which be correſpondent to the vvordes that vvere ſpoken (as the Apoſtle here declareth) in the firſt ſacrifice of the dedication of the old law, hauing alſo expreſſe mention of remiſſion of ſinnes therby, as by the bloud of the new Teſtament. Whereby it is plaine, that the B. Chalice of the altar hath the very ſacrificall bloud in it that vvas ſhed vpon the Croſſe, in & by vvhich, the nevv Teſtament (vvhich is the law of ſpirit, grace, and remiſſion) vvas dedicated, and doth conſiſt. And therfore it is alſo cleere, that many diuine things, vvhich to the Heretikes or ignorant may ſeeme to be ſpoken onely of Chriſtes ſacrifice vpon the Croſſe, be in deede verified & fulfilled alſo in the ſacrifice of the altar. Whereof S. Paul for the cauſes aforeſaid vvould not treat in plaine termes. See Iſychius \+it li. 1 in Leuit. c. 4 paulo poſt initium\+it*, applying al theſe things to the immolation of Chriſt alſo in the Sacrament. \fq ¹ \ft The correspondence of wordes in dedicating both Testaments proueth the real presence of bloud in the Chalice.\ef*
\v 21 The tabernacle alſo & al the veſſel of the miniſterie he in like maner ſprinkled with bloud.
\v 22 And al things almoſt according to the lavv are cleanſed with bloud: and vvithout ſheading of bloud there is not remiſſion.
\v 23 It is neceſſarie therfore that ″ the examplers of the cœleſtials be cleanſed vvith theſe: but the celeſtials them ſelues vvith better hoſtes then theſe. \ef - \fr 9.23 \fq The examplers. \ft ¹ Al the offices, places, veſſels, and inſtruments of the old lavv, vvere but figures and reſemblances of the ſtate and ſacraments of the new Teſtament, vvhich are here called \+it celeſtials\+it*, for that they are the liuely image of the heauenly ſtate next enſuing: vvhich be therfore ſpecially dedicated and ſanctified in Chriſtes bloud, ſacrificed on the altar, and ſprinkled vpon the faithful, as the old figures and people were cleanſed by the bloud of beaſts. And therfore by a tranſition vſual in the holy Scriptures, the Apoſtle ſodenly paſſeth in the ſentence immediatly following and turneth his talke to Chriſtes entrance into heauen, the ſtate vvhereof, both by the Sacraments of the old law, and alſo more ſpecially by them of the new, is prefigured. \fq ¹ \ft In the old Testament were figures of the new: in the new, is resemblance of the heauenly state.\ef*
\v 24 For \sc IESVS\sc* is not entred into Holies made vvith hand, examplers of the true: but into heauen it ſelf, that he may appeare novv to the countenance of God for vs.
\v 25 Nor that he ſhould ″ offer him ſelf often, as the high prieſt entereth into the Holies, euery yere in the bloud of others: \ef - \fr 9.25 \fq Offer him ſelf often. \ft ¹ As Chriſt neuer died but once, nor neuer ſhal die againe, ſo in that violent, painful, and blouddy ſort he can neuer be offered againe, neither needeth he ſo to be offered any more: hauing by that one action of ſacrifice vpon the Croſſe, made the full ranſom, redemption, and remedie for the ſinnes of the vvhole world. Neuertheleſſe, as Chriſt died and was offered after a ſort in all the ſacrifices of the Law and Nature, ſince the beginning of the world (al vvhich vvere figures of this one oblation vpon the Croſſe) ſo is he much rather offered in the sacrifice of the altar of the new Teſtament, incomparably more neerely, diuinely, and truely expreſſing his death, his body broken, his bloud ſhed, then did any figure of the old law, or other ſacrifice that euer was: as being in deede (though in hidden, ſacramental, and myſticall, and vnblouddy maner) the very ſelf ſame B. body and bloud, the ſelf ſame hoſt, oblation and ſacrifice, that was done vpon the Croſſe. ² And this truth is moſt euident by the very forme of vvordes vſed by our Sauiour in the inſtitution and conſecration of the holy Sacrament, and by the profeſſion of all the holy Doctors. \+it Our ſacrifice\+it*, ſaith S. Cyprian, \+it is correſpondent to the Paſſion of Chriſt\+it*. And, \+it The ſacrifice that we offer, is the Paſſion of Chriſt\+it*. ep. 63. nu. 4. & nu, 7. S. Auguſtine de fid. ad Pet. c. 19. \+it In thoſe carnal ſacrifices vvas the prefiguring of the fleſh of Chriſt, vvhich he was to offer for ſinnes, and of the bloud, vvhich he was to ſheade. but in this Sacrifice is the commemoration of the fleſh of Chriſt which he hath now giuen, and of the bloud which he hath ſhed\+it*: in illis prænuntiabatur occidendus, in hoc annuntiatur occiſus. \+it In them he vvas forſhewed as to be killed: in theſe he is ſhewed, as killed\+it*. And S. Gregorie Nazianzene ſaith, \+it orat. in morbum\+it*, that the Prieſt in this ſacrifice, immiſcet ſe magnis Chriſti Paſſionibus. S Ambroſe \+it li. 1 Offic. c. 48, Offertur Chriſtus in imagine quaſi recipiens paſſionem\+it*. Alexander the firſt, \+it ep. ad omnes Orthodox. nu. 4. to. 1. Conc. Cuius corpus & ſanguis conficitur, paſsio etiam celebratur\+it*. S. Gregorie, \+it ho. 37 in Euang. So often as we offer the hoſt of his Paſsion, ſo often we renew his Paſſion\+it*. And, \+it He ſuffereth for vs againe in myſterie\+it*. And Iſychius, \+it li. 2. c. 8 in Leuit. poſt med. By the ſacrifice of the onely-begotten many thinges are giuen vnto vs, to witte, the remiſſion or pardoning of al mankinde, and the ſingular introduction or bringing in of the myſteries of the new Teſtament\+it*. ³ And the ſaid fathers and others, by reaſon of the difference in the maner of Chriſtes preſence and oblation in reſpect of that on the Croſſe, called this \+it the vnblouddy ſacrifice\+it*, as ⁴ Caluin him ſelf confeſſeth, but anſwereth them in the pride of hereticall ſpirit, with theſe wordes: \+it Nihil moror quod ſic loquantur vetuſti ſcriptores\+it*. that is, \+it I paſſe not for it, that the auncient writers do ſo ſpeake\+it*: calling the diſtinction of blouddy and vnblouddy ſacrifice, ſcholaſticall and friuolous, and ⁵ \+it diabolicum commentum, a diueliſh deuiſe\+it*. With ſuch ignorant and blaſphemous men we haue to do, that thinke they vnderſtand the Scriptures better then all the fathers. \fq ¹ \ft Christ once offered in blouddy sort, but vnblouddily often, namely in the sacrifice of the altar. \fq ² \ft The sacrifice of the altar & that on the Crosse, both one. \fq ³ \ft The fathers call it the vnblouddy sacrifice of the altar. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Comment. in 9 Hebr\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Caluins contempt of the fathers.\ef*
\v 26 othervviſe he ought to haue ſuffered often from the beginning of the vvorld: but novv once in the conſummation of the vvorldes, to the deſtruction of ſinne, he hath appeared by his ovvne hoſt.
\v 27 And as it is appointed to men to die once, and after this, the iudgement:
\v 28 ſo alſo Chriſt vvas offered once \f 1 \fr 9.28 \ft By this word which ſignifieth to emptie or draw out euen to the botom, is declared the plentiful and perfect redemption of ſinne by Chriſt.\f*\f 2 \fr 9.28 \ft ad exhaurienda peccata.\f* to exhauſt the ſinnes of many. the ſecond time he ſhal appeare without ſinne to them that expect him, vnto ſaluation.
\c 10
\cd Becauſe in the yerely feaſt of Expiation was only a commemoration of ſinnes, therfore in place of al thoſe old ſacrifices the Pſalme telleth vs of the oblation of Chriſtes body. 10 Which he offered blouddily but once (the Leuitical Prieſts offering ſo euery day) becauſe that once vvas ſufficient for euer, 15 in that it purchaſed (as the prophet alſo witneſſeth) remiſsion of ſinnes. 19 After al this he proſecuteth and exhorteth them vnto perſeuerance, partly vvith the opening of Heauen by our high-prieſt, 26 partly vvith the terrour of damnation if they fall againe: 32 bidding them remember how much they had ſuffered already, and not loſe their reward.
\p
\v 1 \sc FOR\sc* \x - \xt Lev. 16, 14.\x* the lavv hauing a ſhadovv of good things to come, not the very image of the things: euery yere vvith the ſelf ſame hoſtes which they offer inceſſantly, can neuer make the commers thereto perfect: \ef - \fr 10.1 \fq A ſhadow. \ft ¹ The ſacrifices and ceremonies of the old law, vvere ſo far from the truth of Chriſts Sacraments, and from giuing ſpirit, grace, remiſsion, redemption, and iuſtification, and therevpon the entrance into heauen and ioyes celeſtiall, that they vvere but mere ſhadowes, vnperfectly and obſcurely repreſenting the graces of the new Teſtament and of Chriſtes death: whereas all the holy Churches rites and actions inſtituted by Chriſt in the Prieſthod of the new law, conteine and giue grace, iuſtification, and life euerlaſting to the faithful and vvorthy receiuers: and therfore they be not ſhades or darke reſemblances of Chriſtes paſſion, which is the fountaine of all grace and mercie, but perfect images and moſt liuely repreſentations of the ſame, ſpecially the ſacrifice of the altar, which becauſe it is the ſame oblation, the ſame hoſt, and offered by the ſame Prieſt Chriſt \+sc IESVS\+sc* (though by the miniſterie of man and in myſterie) is the moſt pure and neere image, character, and correſpondence to the ſacrifice of Chriſtes paſsion, both in ſubſtance, force, and effect, that can be. \fq ¹ \ft The old sacrifices obscurely shadowed, but the sacrifice of the altar most plainely representeth the sacrifice on the Crosse.\ef*
\v 2 othervviſe they ſhould haue ceaſed to be offered, becauſe the vvorſhippers once cleanſed ſhould haue no conſcience of ſinne any longer. \ef - \fr 10.2 \fq They ſhould haue ceaſed. \ft If the hoſtes and offerings of the old law had been of them ſelues perfect to all effectes of redemption and remiſſion, as the Hebrues (againſt vvhom the Apoſtle diſputeth) did thinke, and had had no relation to Chriſtes ſacrifice on the Croſſe or any other abſolute and vniuerſal oblation or remedie for ſinne, but by and of their owne efficacie could haue generally purged and cleanſed man of all ſinne and damnation: then they ſhould neuer haue needed to be ſo often repeated and reiterated. ¹ For being both, generally auailable for all, by their opinion, and particularly applied (in as ample ſort as they could be) to the ſeueral infirmities of euery offender, there had been no ſinnes left. But ſinnes did remaine, euen thoſe ſinnes for vvhich they had offered ſacrifices before, notwithſtanding their ſacrifices vvere particularly applied vnto them. For, offering yerely they did not onely offer ſacrifices for the new committed crimes, but euen for the old, for vvhich they had often ſacrificed before: the ſacrifices being rather recordes and atteſtations of their ſinnes, then a redemption or full remiſsion, as Chriſtes death is. Which being once applied to man by Baptiſme, wipeth away all ſinnes paſt, God neuer remembring them any more, nor euer any ſacrifice or ſacrament or ceremonie being made or done for them any more, though for new ſinnes other remedies be daily requiſite. Their ſacrifices then could not of them ſelues remitte ſinnes, much leſſe make the general redemption vvithout relation to Chriſtes Paſsion. ² And ſo you ſee it is plaine euery vvhere, that the Apoſtle proueth not by the often repetition of the Iewiſh ſacrifices, that they vvere no ſacrifices at all, but that they vvere not of that abſolute force or efficacie, to make redemption or any remiſsion, without dependance of the one vniuerſal redemption by Chriſt: his whole purpoſe being, to inculcate vnto them the neceſsitie of Chriſtes death, and the oblation of the new Teſtament. As for the Churches holy ſacrifice, it is cleane of an other kinde then thoſe of the Iewes, and therfore he maketh no oppoſition betwixt it, and Chriſtes death or ſacrifice on the Croſſe, in all this Epiſtle: but rather as a ſequele of that one general oblation, couertly alvvaies inferreth the ſame: as being in a different maner the very ſelf ſame hoſt and offering that was done vpon the Croſſe, and continually is vvrought by the ſelf ſame Prieſt. \fq ¹ \ft The Iewes sacrifices were not absolute & independent, because they were often repeated. \fq ² \ft The Apostle proueth by the often repeating of the Iewes sacrifices, not that they were none, but that they were not absolute & sufficient.\ef*
\v 3 but in them there is made a commemoration of ſinne euery yere.
\v 4 for it is impoſſible that vvith the bloud of oxen and goates ſinnes ſhould be taken avvay. \ef - \fr 10.4 \fq Impoſſible. \ft The hoſtes and ſacrifices of the old law, vvhich the carnal Iewes made all the count of, vvithout relation to Chriſtes death, were not onely not perfect and abſolute ſufficient in them ſelues, ¹ but they did not, nor could not remit any ſinnes at all, being but onely ſignes therof, referring the offenders for remiſſion in deede, to Chriſtes Paſſion. For the bloud of bruit beaſtes could haue no other effect, nor any other element or creature, before Chriſtes death. the fruite wherof, before it vvas extant, could be no otherwiſe properly applied vnto them, but by beleefe in him. \fq ¹ \ft The old sacrifices remitted not sinnes, but were only signes therof.\ef*
\v 5 Therfore comming into the vvorld he ſaith: \x - \xt Pſ. 39, 7.\x* \it Hoſt and oblation thou vvouldeſt not: but a body thou haſt fitted to me\it*: \ef - \fr 10.5 \fq Hoſt and oblation. \ft ¹ He meaneth not that God would no hoſt nor ſacrifice any more, as the Proteſtants falſely imagin: for that were to take away not onely the ſacrifice of Chriſtes body vpon the altar, but the ſacrifice of the ſame body vpon the Croſſe alſo. Therfore the Prophet ſpeaketh onely of the legal and carnal ſacrifices of the Iewes, ſignifying that they did neuer of them ſelues pleaſe God, but in reſpect of Chriſt, by whoſe oblation of his owne body they ſhould pleaſe. \fq ¹ \ft God refuseth the Iewes sacrifices, not al sacrifice.\ef* \ef - \fr 10.5 \fq But a body. \ft ¹ If Chriſt had not had a body, he could not haue had any vvorthy matter or any matter at all to ſacrifice in viſible maner, other then the hoſtes of the old law. Neither could he either haue made the general redemption by his one oblation vpon the Croſſe, nor the daily ſacrifice of the Church: for both which, his body was fitted by the diuine wiſedom. Which is an high concluſion, not vnderſtood of Iewes, Pagans, nor the Heretikes of our time, that Chriſtes humane nature was taken to make the Sonne of God (who in his diuine nature could not be either Prieſt or hoſt) fitte to be the ſacrifice and Prieſt of his father, in a more vvorthy ſort, then all the Prieſts or oblations of the old law. ² And that this body vvas giuen him, not onely to be the ſacrifice vpon the Croſſe, but alſo vpon the altar, S. Auguſtine affirmeth in theſe vvordes. \+it The table which the Prieſt of the new Teſtament doth exhibit, is of his body and bloud: for that is the ſacrifice which ſucceded al theſe ſacrifices that were offered in ſhadow of that to come. For the which alſo we acknovvledge that voice of the ſame Mediatour in the pſalme\+it*, ³ \+sc BVT\+sc* A \+sc BODY THOV HAST FITTED TO ME\+sc*, \+it becauſe in ſteede of all thoſe ſacrifices and oblations his body is offered, and is miniſtred to the partakers or receiuers. Li. 17 Ciuit. Dei. c. 20\+it*. And againe \+it li. 4 de Trin. c. 14. Who ſo iuſt and holy a Prieſt, as the onely ſonne of God? What might ſo conueniently be offered for men, of men, as mans fleſh? and vvhat ſo fitte for this immolation or offering, as mortal fleſh? what ſo cleane for cleanſing the vices of mortal men, as the fleſh borne of the virgins wombe? and vvhat can be offered and receiued ſo gratefully, as the fleſh of our ſacrifice, made the body of our Prieſt\+it*? \fq ¹ \ft That Christ should haue a body, was necessarie for his Priesthod, and sacrifice. \fq ² \ft The body of Christ is the sacrifice of the altar. \fq ³ \ft \+it Ps. 39\+it*.\ef*
\v 6 \it Holocauſtes and \it* \f 1 \fr 10.6 \ft \+it For ſinne\+it*, is the proper name of a certaine ſacrifice called in Hebrue חטאת: as Holocauſt is an other kinde. \+it See the Annot. 2 Cor. 5. v. 21\+it*.\f* \it for ſinne did not pleaſe thee\it*.
\v 7 \it Then ſaid I, Behold I come: in the head of the booke it is vvritten of me: That I may doe thy vvil ô God\it*.
\v 8 Saying before, \it Becauſe hoſtes and oblations, & holocauſtes, & for ſinne thou vvouldeſt not, neither did they pleaſe thee\it*, vvhich are offered according to the lavv, \ef - \fr 10.8 \fq Neither did they pleaſe thee. \ft ¹ By that he ſaith, the things offered in the Lavv, did not pleaſe God, and likevviſe by that he ſaith, the former to be taken avvay, that the ſecond may haue place, it is euident, that all hoſtes and ſacrifices be not taken avvay by Chriſt, as the Heretikes foliſhly conceiue: but that the old hoſtes of brute beaſtes be abrogated to giue place to that which is the proper hoſt of the new law, that is, Chriſtes owne body. \fq ¹ \ft The Iewes sacrifices refused, not al sacrifice.\ef*
\v 9 \it then ſaid I, Behold I come that I may doe thy vvil ô God\it*: he taketh avvay the firſt, that he may eſtabliſh that that folovveth.
\v 10 In the vvhich vvil, vve are ſanctified by the oblation of the body of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt once.
\v 11 And euery prieſt in deede is ready daily miniſtring, and often offering the ſame hoſtes, vvhich can neuer take avvay ſinnes: \ef - \fr 10.11 \fq Often offering the ſame hoſtes. \ft ¹ As S. Paul is forced often to inculcate that one principle of the efficacie and ſufficiencie of Chriſt's death, becauſe of the Hebrews' too much attributing to their legal ſacrifices, and for that they did not referre them to Chriſt's only oblation: ſo vve, through the intolerable ignorance and importunity of the Heretics of this time (abuſing the words of the Apoſtle ſpoken in the devve defence and declaration of the value and efficacie of Chriſt's Paſſion aboue the ſacrifices of the Lavv) are forced to repeat often, that the Apoſtle's reaſon of many Prieſts and often repetition of the ſelf ſame ſacrifices, concerneth the ſacrifices of the Law only, vnto which he oppoſeth Chriſt's ſacrifice and Prieſthod: and ſpeaketh no vvord of or againſt the Sacrifice of the new Teſtament, vvhich is the ſacrifice of Chriſt's owne Prieſthod, Law, and inſtitution, yea the ſame ſacrifice done daily unbloodily, that once was done bloodily: made by the ſame Prieſt Chriſt Jeſus, though by his miniſters' hands: and not many hoſts, as thoſe of the old law were, but the very ſelf ſame in number, euen Chriſt's owne body that vvas crucified. And that you may ſee that this is the iudgement of all antiquity, and their expoſition of theſe and the like words of this Epiſtle, and that they ſeeing the very ſame arguments that the Proteſtants novv make ſo much a do vvithall among the ſimple and vnlearned, yet vvel perceiued that they made nothing againſt the daily oblation or ſacrifice of the altar, and therefore anſvvered them before the Proteſtants vvere extant, 1200 years ²: vve will ſet dovvne ſome of their words, whoſe authoritie and expoſition of the Scriptures muſt preuaile in all that haue wiſdom or the feare of God, aboue the falſe and vaine gloſſes of Caluin and his followers. Thus then firſt ſaith S. Ambroſe ³: \+it Quid ergo nos &c. What vve then? Do not vve offer euery day? We offer ſurely: but this ſacrifice is an exampler of that: for we offer always the ſelf ſame, and not now one lambe, tomorrow another, but always the ſelf ſame thing: therefore it is one ſacrifice. othervviſe, by this reaſon becauſe it is offered in many places, there ſhould be many Chriſts: not ſo, but it is one Chriſt in euery place, here whole, and there whole, one body. But this which vve do, is done for a commemoration of that which was done. For we offer not another ſacrifice, as the high Prieſt of the old law, but always the ſelf-ſame. &c.\+it* Primaſius ⁴ S. Auguſtine's ſcholar doth alſo preoccupate theſe Proteſtants' obiections thus: \+it What ſhal vve ſay then? Do not our Prieſts daily offer ſacrifice? They offer ſurely, because vve ſinne daily, and daily haue neede to be cleanſed: and becauſe he can not die, he hath giuen vs the ſacrament of his body and blood: that as his Paſsion was the redemption and abſolution of the vvorld, ſo alſo this oblation may be redemption and cleanſing to all that offer it in truth and veritie.\+it* So ſaith this holy Father, to vvitte, ⁵ that as the ſacrifice of the Croſs was a general redemption, ſo this of the altar is, to all that vſe it, a particular redemption or application of Chriſt's redemption to them. In which ſenſe alſo V. Bede calleth the holy Maſs, \+it redemptionem corporis & animæ ſempiternam, the euerlaſting redemption of body and ſoul.\+it* li. 4. c. 22. hiſtor. Againe the ſame Primaſius ⁶, \+it The diuinity of the Word of God which is euery-vvhere, maketh that there are not many ſacrifices, but one, although it be offered of many, and that as it is one body vvhich he tooke of the Virgin's vvombe, not many bodies, euen ſo alſo one ſacrifice, not diuers, as thoſe of the Jews were.\+it* ⁷ S. Chryſoſtom alſo, and after him Theophylacte, and Oecumenius, and of the Latins, Haimo, Paſchaſius, Remigius, and others, obiect to themſelves thus: \+it Do not vve alſo offer euery day? vve offer ſurely, but this ſacrifice is an exampler of that, for vve offer always the ſelf ſame: and not novv one lambe, tomorrow another, but the ſelf-ſame: therefore this is one ſacrifice. Othervviſe, becauſe it is offered in many places, there ſhould be many Chriſts. And a litle after, not another ſacrifice, as the high Prieſt of the old Law, but the ſelf ſame we do always offer, rather vvorking a remembrance or commemoration of the ſacrifice.\+it* See the Annotation Luke 22, 19. vpon theſe words, \+it A commemoration\+it*. Thus did all the ancient Fathers Greeke and Latin treate of theſe matters, and ſo they ſaid Maſs, and offered daily, and many of them made ſuch forms of celebrating the diuine ſacrifice, as the Greeks and Latins do vſe in their Liturgies and Maſſes, and yet they ſavv theſe places of the Apoſtle and made commentaries vpon them, and vnderſtood them (I trow) as vvel as the Proteſtants. He that for his further confirmation or comfort liſt ſee vvhat the ancient ⁸ Councels and Doctors believed, taught, and practiſed in this thing, let him read the firſt holy Councel of Nice \+it cap. 14. & in fine Conc. ex Græco.\+it* the Councel of Epheſus Anathematiſ. 11. the Chalcedon Councel \+it act. 3. pag. 112. Conc. Ancyran. c. 1. 4. & 5.\+it* Neocæſar. \+it can. 13.\+it* Laodic. \+it can. 19.\+it* Carthag. \+it 2 cap. 8.\+it* Carthag. \+it 3. cap. 24.\+it* & Carthag. \+it 4. cap. 33. & c. 41.\+it* S. Denys \+it cap. 3. Eccl. hier.\+it* S. Andrevve \+it in hiſtoria Paſſionis\+it*. S. Ignatius \+it ep. ad Smyrnenſes.\+it* S. Martialis \+it ep. ad Burdegalenſes.\+it* S. Iuſtine \+it Dialog. cum Triphone.\+it* S. Irenæus \+it li. 4. cap. 32. 34.\+it* Tertullian \+it de cultu foeminarum, & de corona milit.\+it* Origen \+it homil. 13. in Levit.\+it* S. Cyprian \+it ep. ad Cecilium. nu. 2. & de Cæna Domini. nu. 14. &\+it* Euſebius \+it demonſt. Evang. li. 1. cap. 10.\+it* and the reſt which vve haue cited by occaſion before, & might cite but for tediouſnes: a truth moſt known and agreed vpon in the Chriſtian religion. \fq ¹ \ft We must often note that the Apostles speache of many Priests and often sacrificing, concerneth only the Iewes Priests and sacrifices, not the Priests and sacrifice of the new Testament. \fq ² \ft The Caluinists arguments against Christs body often offered, and in many places, answered by the fathers long a goe. \fq ³ \ft \+it in 10 cap. Hebr\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it ibidem\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft The general redemption vpon the Crosse is particularly applied in the sacrifice of the altar. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Primas. loco citato\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft * \+it ho. 17 in ep. ad Heb\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft Councels and Fathers.\ef*
\v 12 but this man offering one hoſt for ſinnes, for euer \x - \xt Ps. 109. 1. Cor. 15, 25.\x* ſitteth on the right hand of God,
\v 13 hence forth expecting, vntil his enemies be put the footeſtoole of his feete.
\v 14 For by one oblation hath he conſummated for euer them that are ſanctified.
\v 15 And the holy Ghoſt alſo doth teſtifie to vs. For after that he ſaid:
\v 16 \x - \xt Hier. 31, 33, 34. Heb. 8, 8.\x* \it And this is the Teſtament vvhich I wil make to them after thoſe daies, ſaith our Lord, giuing my lavves \it* \f 2 \fr 10.16 \ft Heb. 8, 8. · This is partly fulfilled in & by the grace of the new teſtament, but it ſhal be perfectly accompliſhed in heauen.\f* \it in their hartes, & in their\it* mindes vvil I ſuperſcribe them:
\v 17 \it and their ſinnes and iniquities I wil now remember no more\it*.
\v 18 But vvhere there is remiſſion of theſe, novv there is not an oblation for ſinnes. \ef - \fr 10.18 \fq Now there is not. \ft ¹ Chriſtes death can not be applied vnto vs in that full and ample ſort as it is in baptiſme, but once: Chriſt appointing that large remiſſion and application to be made but once in euery man, as Chriſt died but once. for it is not meant, that all ſinne ſhal ceaſe after Chriſtes ſacrifice vpon the Croſſe, nor that there ſhould be no oblation for ſinnes committed after Baptiſme, or that a man could not ſinne at al after Baptiſme, or that if he ſinned aftervvard, he could haue no remedie or remiſſion by Gods ordinance in the Church, which diuers falſehods ſundrie Heretikes gather of this and ſuch like places: but onely the Apoſtle telleth the Hebrues, as he did before chap. 6, and as he doth ſtraight aftervvard, that if they fall now (wherevnto they ſeemed very prone) to their old law, and voluntarily after the knowledge and profeſſion of the Chriſtian faith by Baptiſme, commit this ſinne of incredulitie and apoſtaſie, they can neuer haue that aboundant remiſſion applied vnto them by Baptiſme, vvhich can neuer be miniſtred to them againe. And that general full pardon he calleth here, \+it oblation\+it*, and afterward in the 26 verſe, \+it hoſtiam pro peccato, an hoſt for ſinne\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft When the Apostle seemeth to say, there is no remission or oblation for sinne, he alwaies meaneth that ful remission by Baptisme.\ef*
\v 19 Hauing therfore brethren confidence in the entring of the holies in the bloud of Chriſt:
\v 20 vvhich \f 1 \fr 10.20 \ft To dedicat, is to be authour & beginner of a thing. The Proteſtants tranſlate, \+it he hath prepared\+it*, for their hereſie that Chriſt was not the firſt man that entered into heauen.\f*\f 2 \fr 10.20 \ft \+it c ἐνεκαίνισεν\+it*\f* he hath cdedicated to vs a nevv and liuing vvay by the vele, that is, his fleſh,
\v 21 and a high prieſt ouer the houſe of God,
\v 22 let vs approche vvith a true hart in fulneſſe of faith, hauing our hartes ſprinkled from euil conſcience, and our body waſhed vvith cleane vvater,
\v 23 let vs hold the confeſſion of our hope vndeclining (for he is faithful that hath promiſed)
\v 24 and let vs conſider one an other vnto the prouocation of charitie and of good vvorkes:
\v 25 not forſaking our aſſemblie as ſome are accuſtomed, but comforting, and ſo much the more as you ſee the day approching.
\v 26 For \x - \xt Heb. 6, 4.\x* if vve ſinne vvillingly after the knovvledge of the truth receiued, novv there is not left an hoſt for ſinnes, \ef - \fr 10.26 \fq If we ſinne vvillingly. \ft ¹ As the Caluiniſts abuſe other like places againſt the holy ſacrifice of the Maſſe, ſo they abuſe this as the Nouatians did before them, to proue that an Heretike, Apoſtata, or any that wilfully forſaketh the truth, can neuer be forgiuen. Vvhich (as is before declared in the 6 chapter) is moſt wicked blaſphemie: the meaning hereof being, as is there ſaid, onely to terrifie the Hebrues, ² that falling from Chriſt they can not ſo eaſily haue the hoſt of Chriſtes death applied vnto them, becauſe they can not be baptized any more, but muſt paſſe by ſacramental penance, and ſatisfaction, and other hard remedies vvhich Chriſt hath preſcribed after Baptiſme in the Churches diſcipline. Therfore S. Cyril ſaith, \+it li. 5 in Io. cap. 17. Penance is not excluded by theſe wordes of Paul, but the renevving by the lauer of regeneration. He doth not here take away the ſecond or third remiſsion of ſinnes (for he is not ſuch an enemy to our ſaluation) but the hoſt which is Chriſt he denieth that it is to be offered againe vpon the Croſſe\+it*. So ſaith this holy Doctor. And by this place & the like you ſee, ³ how perilous a thing it is for Heretikes & ignorant perſons to read the Scriptures. Which by folovving their owne fantaſie ⁴ they peruert to their damnation. \fq ¹ \ft The Caluinists heresie against remission of sinnes. \fq ² \ft Al sinnes may be remitted by penance, but not so fully as by Baptisme. \fq ³ \ft Perilous reading of the Scriptures. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 2 Pet. 3\+it*.\ef*
\v 27 but a certaine terrible expectation of iudgement and rage of fire, vvhich ſhal conſume the aduerſaries.
\v 28 A man making the lavv of Moyſes fruſtrate: vvithout any mercie \x - \xt Deu. 19, 15. Mat. 18, 16. Io. 8, 17.\x* dieth vnder tvvo or three vvitneſſes.
\v 29 \f 1 \fr 10.29 \ft Hereſie and Apoſtaſie from the Catholike Faith, puniſhable by death.\f* how much more thinke you, doth he deſerue vvorſe puniſhements vvhich hath troden the ſonne of God vnder foote, and eſtemed the bloud of the teſtament polluted, vvherein he is ſanctified, and hath done contumelie to the ſpirit of grace? \ef - \fr 10.29 \fq The bloud of the Teſtament. \ft ¹ Vvhoſoeuer maketh no more account of the bloud of Chriſtes ſacrifice, either as ſhed vpon the Croſſe, or as in the holy Chalice of the altar (for our Sauiour calleth that alſo ² the bloud of the new Teſtament) then he doth of the bloud of calues and goates, or of other common drinkes, is vvorthy death, and God vvil in the next life, if it be not puniſhed here, reuenge it vvith greuous puuiſhment. \fq ¹ \ft Contempt of Christs bloud in the Sacrament. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 22\+it*.\ef*
\v 30 For vve knovv him that ſaid, \x - \xt Deu. 32, 35. Rom. 12, 19.\x* \it Reuenge to me, I wil repay\it*. And againe, \x - \xt Ps. 134, 14.\x* \it That our Lord vvil iudge his people\it*.
\v 31 It is horrible to fal into the handes of the liuing God. \ef - \fr 10.31 \fq It is horrible. \ft ¹ Let al Chriſtian people do ſatisfaction and penance for their ſinnes in this life. for the iudgements of God in the next life done by God him ſelf, of what ſort ſoeuer, whether temporall as in Purgatorie, or eternal as in Hell, be exceding greuous. \fq ¹ \ft Penance.\ef*
\v 32 \f 1 \fr 10.32 \ft The Epiſtle for many Martyrs.\f* But call to minde the old daies: vvherein being illuminated, you ſuſtained a great fight of paſſions.
\v 33 and on the one part certes by reproches and tribulations made a ſpectacle: and on the other part made companions of them that conuerſed in ſuch ſort.
\v 34 For, you both had compaſſion on them that vvere in bondes: and the ſpoile of your ovvne goodes you tooke vvith ioy, knovving that you haue a better and a permanent ſubſtance. \ef - \fr 10.34 \fq You had compaſſion. \ft ¹ To be merciful to the afflicted for religion, & to be partakers of their miſeries, is a very meritorious worke, and giueth great confidence before God in the day of repaiment or remuneration for the ſame. \fq ¹ \ft Mercie to the emprisoned for religion.\ef* \ef - \fr 10.34 \fq With ioy. \ft ¹ If all Chriſtian men would conſider this, they would not thinke it ſo great a matter to loſe their land or goods for defenſe of the Catholike faith. \fq ¹ \ft Losse of goods for religion.\ef*
\v 35 Do not therfore leeſe your \f 1 \fr 10.35 \ft Good workes make great confidence of ſaluation, & haue great revvard.\f* confidence, vvhich hath a great remuneration.
\v 36 For patience is neceſſarie for you: that doing the vvil of God, you may receiue the promiſe.
\v 37 For \x - \xt Abac. 2, 3. Ro. 1, 17. Gal. 3, 12.\x* yet a litle and a very litle vvhile, he that is to come, vvil come, and vvil not ſlacke.
\v 38 and my iuſt liueth of faith. ⊢ but if he vvithdravv him ſelf, he ſhal not pleaſe my ſoule. \ef - \fr 10.38 \fq Liueth of faith. \ft ¹ Faithful men afflicted in this life, haue their comfort in their aſſured faith and hope of Chriſtes comming to deliuer them once from all theſe miſeries: & ſo by that faith and comfort they liue, whereas otherwiſe this miſerable life were a death. \fq ¹ \ft Faith is the comfort of the afflicted.\ef*
\v 39 But vve are not the children of vvithdravving vnto perdition: but of faith to the vvinning of the ſoule.
\c 11
\cd He exhorteth them by the definition of faith, to ſticke vnto God, though they ſee not yet his revvard: ſhewing that all the Saincts aforetime did the like, being all conſtant in faith, though not one of them receiued the promis, that is, the inheritance in heauen: but they and we now after the comming of Chriſt receiue it together.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* faith is, \f 1 \fr 11.1 \ft By this word ſubſtance is meant, that faith is the ground of our hope.\f* the ſubſtance of things to be hoped for, \f 2 \fr 11.1 \ft \+it c ἔλεγχος.\+it*\f* the argument of things not appearing. \ef - \fr 11.1 \fq Faith is. \ft ¹ By this deſcription of faith, and by all the commendation therof through the whole chapter, you may vvell perceiue that the Apoſtle knewe not the forged ſpecial faith of the Proteſtants, vvhereby euery one of theſe new Sectmaſters & their folovvers beleeue their ſinnes are remitted, and that them ſelues ſhal be ſaued, though their ſectes be cleane contrarie one to an other. \fq ¹ \ft Not only or a special faith.\ef* \ef - \fr 11.1 \fq Not appearing. \ft ¹ \+it This is the praiſe of faith\+it*, ſaith S. Auguſtine, \+it if that which is beleeued, be not ſeen. For vvhat great thing is it, if that be beleeued, which is ſeen? according to that ſentence of our Lord when he rebuked his diſciple, ſaying: Becauſe thou haſt ſeen me Thomas, thou haſt beleeued: bleſſed are they that haue not ſeen and haue beleeued. Aug. in euang. Io. tract. 79\+it*. Which may be a rebuke alſo and a checke to al thoſe faithles ſpeaches, I would ſee him, taſt him, touch him and feele his very fleſh in the Sacrament, othervviſe I wil not beleeue. \fq ¹ \ft Faith is of things not seen: as in the B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 2 For in this the old men obtained teſtimonie.
\v 3 \x - \xt Gen. 1, 13.\x* By faith, vve vnderſtand that the vvorldes vvere framed by the vvord of God: that of inuiſible things viſible things might be made.
\v 4 \x - \xt Gen. 4, 4.\x* By faith, Abel offered a greater hoſte to God then Cain: \x - \xt Mat. 23, 35.\x* by vvhich he obtained teſtimonie that he vvas iuſt, God giuing teſtimonie to his giftes, & by it, he being dead, yet ſpeaketh.
\v 5 \x - \xt Gen. 5, 24. Eccl. 44, 16.\x* By faith \f 2 \fr 11.5 \ft Here it appeareth that Henoch yet liueth and is not dead: againſt the Caluiniſts. See the annot. \+it Apocal. chap. 11\+it*. · Gen. 5, 24. Eccl. 44, 16.\f* Henoch vvas tranſlated, that he ſhould not ſee death, and he vvas not found: becauſe God tranſlated him. for before his tranſlation he had teſtimonie that he had pleaſed God.
\v 6 But vvithout faith it is impoſſible to pleaſe God. For he that commeth to God, muſt beleeue that he is, and is a \f 1 \fr 11.6 \ft We muſt beleeue that God will reward all our good workes: for he is a rewarder of true iuſtice, not an accepter or imputer of that that is not.\f* revvarder to them that ſeeke him. \ef - \fr 11.6 \fq He that commeth. \ft ¹ Faith is the foundation and ground of all other vertues and worſhip of God, vvithout which no man can pleaſe God. Therfore if one be a Iewe, a Heathen, or an heretike, that is to ſay, be without the Catholike faith, al his workes ſhal profit him no whit to ſaluation. \fq ¹ \ft Nothing profitable or meritorious without faith,\ef*
\v 7 \x - \xt Gen. 6, 13. Eccl. 44, 17.\x* By faith, Noë hauing receiued an anſvver concerning thoſe things vvhich as yet vvere not ſeen, fearing, framed the arke for the ſauing of his houſe, by the vvhich he condemned the vvorld: and vvas inſtituted heire of the iuſtice vvhich is by faith.
\v 8 \x - \xt Gen. 12, 4. 13, 1.\x* By faith, he that is called, Abraham, obeied to goe forth into the place vvhich he vvas to receiue for inheritance: and he vvent forth, not knovving whither he vvent.
\v 9 By faith, he abode in the land of promiſe, as in a ſtrange land, dvvelling in cottages vvith Iſaac & Iacob the coheires of the ſame promiſe.
\v 10 For he expected the citie that hath foundations: vvhoſe artificer and maker is God.
\v 11 \x - \xt Gen. 17, 19. 18, 10. 21, 2. Eccli. 44. 22.\x* By faith, Sara alſo her ſelf being barren, receiued vertue in conceauing of ſeede, yea paſt the time of age: becauſe ſhe beleeued that he vvas faithful which had promiſed.
\v 12 For the vvhich cauſe euen of one (and him quite dead) there roſe as the ſtarres of heauen in multitude, & as the ſand that is by the ſea ſhore innumerable.
\v 13 According to faith died al theſe, not hauing receiued the promiſes, but beholding them a farre of, and ſaluting them, and confeſſing that they are pilgrimes & ſtrangers vpon the earth,
\v 14 for they that ſay theſe things, doe ſignifie that they ſeeke a countrie.
\v 15 And in deede if they had been mindeful of the ſame from vvhence they came forth, they had time verely to returne.
\v 16 but novv they deſire a better, that is to ſay, a heauenly. Therfore God is not confounded to be called their God. for he hath prepared them a citie.
\v 17 \x - \xt Gen. 22, 9.\x* By faith, Abraham offered Iſaac, vvhen he vvas tempted: and his onlie-begotten did he offer vvho had receiued the promiſes:
\v 18 (to vvhom it vvas ſaid, \x - \xt Gen. 21, 12. Rom. 9, 7.\x* \it That in Iſaac ſhal ſeede be called to thee\it*.)
\v 19 accounting that God is able to raiſe vp euen from the dead. wherevpon he receiued him alſo \f 1 \fr 11.19 \ft That is, in figure and myſterie of Chriſt dead, & aliue againe.\f* for a parable.
\v 20 \x - \xt Gen. 27, 27. 36.\x* By faith, alſo of things to come, Iſaac bleſſed Iacob and Eſau.
\v 21 \x - \xt Gen. 48, 15.\x* By faith, Iacob dying, bleſſed euery one of the ſonnes of Ioſeph: \x - \xt Gen. 47, 31.\x* and adored the toppe of his rodde. \ef - \fr 11.21 \fq Adored the toppe of his rod. \ft ¹ The learned may ſee here that the Apoſtle doth not tie him ſelf to the Hebrew in the place of Geneſis ² whence it is alleged, but folovveth the Septuaginta, though it differ from the Hebrew, as alſo the other Apoſtles and Euangeliſts and our Sauiour himſelf did: neither vvere they curious (as men now a days) to examine all by the Hebrew only, becauſe they vvriting and ſpeaking by the Holy Ghoſt, knevve very well that this tranſlation ³ is the ſenſe of the Holy Ghoſt alſo, and as true, and as directly intended as the other: and therefore alſo that tranſlation continued always authentical in the Greeke Church, notwithſtanding the diuerſitie thereof from the Hebrew. Euen ſo vve that be Catholics, folow vvith all the Latin fathers the authentical ⁴ Latin tranſlation, though it be not always agreable to the Hebrew or Greeke that now is. But Caluin is not only very ſaucie, but very ignorant, vvhen he ſaith that the Septuaginta were deceiued, and yet that the Apoſtle without curioſity vvas content to folow them: becauſe it is euident, that ⁵ the Hebrew being then without points, ⁶ might be tranſlated the one way as vvel as the other. Vvhich they vnderſtood ſo vvel (and therefore vvere not deceiued) that within three lines after, in the beginning of the next chapter, they tranſlate the ſame vvord, as he vvould haue it in this place. ⁷ Againe obſerue in thoſe words, \+it He adored the toppe of his rod,\+it* that adoration (as the Scripture vſeth this word) may be done to creatures, or to God at and before a creature: as, at or ⁸ before the Arke of the Teſtament in old time, now at or before the crucifixe, relics, images: and in the Pſalms 98. 131. \+it Adore ye his footſtool. Adore ye toward his holy mount. We will adore tovvard the place where his feete ſtoode\+it*: or (vvhich by the Hebrew phraſe is all one) \+it Adore ye his holy mount. We will adore the place where his feete ſtood.\+it* as alſo ⁹ the Greeke Fathers, S. Damaſcene \+it li. 1. de imaginibus\+it*, and Leontius cited of him, yea S. Chryſoſtom alſo do handle theſe places, and namely that of the Apoſtle vvhich vve now ſpeake of, interpreting the Greeke as our Latin hath, and as we do, He adored the rod or the toppe of his rod, that is, the ſcepter of Joſeph now Prince of Ægypt, ſo fulfilling Ioſephs dreams which foretold the ſame \+it Gen. 37\+it*: and vvithal ſignifying as it vvere by this propheticall fact, ¹⁰ the kingdom of Iſrael or of the ten tribes that was to come of Joſeph by Ephraim his younger ſonne in the firſt king Jeroboam, thus the Greeke Fathers. Vvherevnto may be added, that all this vvas done in type and figure of Chriſt's ſcepter and kingdom, whom he adored by and in his Croſs, as he did Joſeph by or in his rod and ſcepter: and therefore the Apoſtle ſaith, he did it by faith, as hauing reſpect tovvard things to come. By all vvhich it is euident, that it is falſe vvhich the Caluiniſts teach, that vve may not adore image, crucifixe, or any viſible creature, that is, we may not adore God at or by ſuch creatures, nor kneele before them: and therefore their ¹¹ corrupt tranſlation of this place for the ſame purpoſe is intolerable, ſaying thus, (\+sc Leaning\+sc*) \+it vpon his ſtaffe he adored\+it* (\+sc God\+sc*). adding no leſſe then tvvo words more then is in the Greeke. Which though it might be the ſenſe of the place, and S. Auguſtine ſo expoundeth it, yet they ſhould not make his expoſition the text of holy Scripture, ſpecially whereas he only of all the ancient Fathers (as Beza confeſſeth) ſo expoundeth it. \fq ¹ \ft The citations in the new Testament, not only according to the Hebrue, but to the Septuaginta. \fq ² \ft \+it Gen. 47. v. 31\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it Aug. de ciu. Dei li. 15 c. 14\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft The vulgar latin translation. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it מטה\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it * ῥάβδος, rod. κλίνη, bed.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Adoration of creatures, and namely of holy things. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it * Ios. 7, 6.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Oecum. in collect\+it*. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it 3 Reg. 11. 12\+it*. \fq ¹¹ \ft Corrupt translation against \+it Dulia\+it*.\ef*
\v 22 \x - \xt Gen. 50, 24. 25.\x* By faith, Ioſeph dying, made mention of the going forth of the children of Iſraël: and gaue commaundement \f 2 \fr 11.22 \ft Gen. 50, 24, 25. · The tranſlation of Relikes or Saincts bodies, & the due regard and honour vve ought to haue to the ſame, are proued hereby.\f* concerning his bones.
\v 23 \x - \xt Exo. 2, 2.\x* By faith, Moyſes being borne, vvas hidde three monethes by his parents: becauſe they ſavv him a proper infant, and they feared not \x - \xt Exo. 1, 16.\x* the kings edict.
\v 24 \x - \xt Exo. 2, 11.\x* By faith, Moyſes being made great, denied him ſelf to be the ſonne of Pharaos daughter:
\v 25 rather choſing to be afflicted vvith the people of God, then to haue the pleaſure of temporal ſinne,
\v 26 eſteeming the reproche of Chriſt, greater riches then the treaſure of the Ægyptians. for \f 1 \fr 11.26 \ft The Proteſtants that deny we may or ought to doe good in reſpect or for revvard in heauen, are hereby confuted.\f* he looked vnto the remuneration.
\v 27 \x - \xt Exo. 12, 37.\x* By faith, he left Ægypt: not fearing the fiercenes of the king. for him that is inuiſible he ſuſteined as if he had ſeen him.
\v 28 By faith, he celebrated the Paſche, & the ſheading of the bloud: that he which deſtroied the firſt-borne, might not touche them.
\v 29 \x - \xt Gen. 14, 22.\x* By faith they paſſed the redde ſea as it vvere by the drie land: vvhich the Ægyptians aſſaying, vvere deuoured.
\v 30 \x - \xt Ios, 6, 20.\x* By faith the vvalles of Iericho fel dovvne, by the circuting of ſeuen daies.
\v 31 \x - \xt Ios. 6, 23. 25. 2, 3.\x* By faith, Rahab the harlot periſhed not vvith the incredulous, receiuing the ſpies vvith peace.
\v 32 And vvhat ſhal I yet ſay? For the time vvil faile me telling of Gedeon, Barac, Sampſon, Iephtè, Dauid, Samuël, & the prophets:
\v 33 \f 1 \fr 11.33 \ft The Epiſtle for SS. Fabian and Sebaſtian Ian. 20. S. Symphoroſa cum ſeptem filiis. Iul. 18. And for many Martyrs.\f* vvho by faith ouercame kingdoms, vvrought iuſtice, obteined promiſes, ſtopped the mouthes of lions, \ef - \fr 11.33 \fq Wrought iuſtice. \ft ¹ Men are not iuſt by beleefe onely, as the Proteſtants affirme, but by working iuſtice. And vve may note that in all this long commendation of faith in the fathers and holy perſons, their good workes are alſo ſpecially recounted, as Rahabs harbouring the ſpies, Abrahams offering his ſonne (vvhich their vvorkes S. Iames ² doth inculcate:) Noës making the Arke \+it Gen. 6\+it*. Abels better oblation then Cains \+it Gen. 4. & Hebr. 11. v. 4\+it*. and ſo forth. therfore S. Clement Alexandrinus ſaith, ³that the ſaid perſons and others vvere iuſt by faith and obedience, by faith and hoſpitality, by faith and patience, by faith and humility. ⁴ The Apoſtles purpoſe then is nothing els, but to proue to the Hebrues (vvho made ſo great account of their Patriarches and forefathers and their famous actes) that all theſe glorious perſonages and their vvorkes vvere commendable and acceptable onely through the faith they had of Chriſt, vvithout vvhich faith none of all their liues and workes ſhould haue profited them any whit: the Gentiles doing many noble actes (as Heretikes may alſo doe) vvhich are of no eſtimation before God, becauſe they lacke faith. And that is the ſcope of S. Paules Epiſtle to the Romanes, and of al other paſſages where he commendeth faith: further prouing ſpecially in this Epiſtle to the Hebrues, that all their ſacrifices vvere nothing els but figures and atteſtations of the Chriſtian faith in Chriſt and his death. Al vvhich high reſolution & concluſion againſt the Iewes and Gentiles, that the Chriſtian faith is the true faith & religion, the Heretikes of our time ignorantly and brutiſhly abuſe againſt Chriſtian workes, ſacrifice, and Sacraments, which the Apoſtle meant ſpecially to commend and eſtabliſh by his high commendation of the faith in Chriſt. \fq ¹ \ft Not faith onely. \fq ² \ft \+it Iac. 2.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Li. 4 Stro. pag. 240\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft No workes of the Patriarkes or any other profitable, but by their faith in Christ. Which is alwaies the Apostles meaning in commending faith.\ef*
\v 34 extinguiſhed the force of fire, repelled the edge of the ſword, recouered of their infirmitie, vvere made ſtrong in battel, turned avvay the campe of forainers:
\v 35 vvomen receiued of reſurrection their dead. and others vvere racked, not accepting redemption, that they might finde a better reſurrection.
\v 36 And others had trial of mockeries and ſtripes, moreouer alſo of bandes & priſons:
\v 37 they vvere ſtoned, they vvere hevved, they vvere tempted, they died in the ſlaughter of the ſword, they vvent about in ſheep-ſkinnes, in goates ſkinnes, needy, in diſtreſſe, afflicted:
\v 38 of vvhom the vvorld vvas not vvorthie. vvandering in deſertes, in mountaines and dennes, and in caues of the earth.
\v 39 And al theſe being approued by the teſtimonie of faith, ⊢ receiued not the promiſe,
\v 40 God for vs prouiding ſome better thing, that they vvithout vs ſhould not be conſummate. \ef - \fr 11.40 \fq Without vs ſhould not. \ft ¹ The fathers before Chriſt could not be accompliſhed, that is, not admitted to the heauenly ioyes, viſion, and fruition of God, till the Apoſtles and other of the new law vvere aſſociate to them, and the vvay to euerlaſting glorie opened by our Lordes death and Aſcenſion. Neither ſhal either they or vve be fully perfected in glorie both of body and ſoul, till the general reſurrection: Gods prouidence being ſo, that vve ſhould not one be conſummated vvithout an other, all being of one faith, and redeemed by one Lord Chriſt. \fq ¹ \ft The Patriarkes and other iust not in heauen before Christ.\ef*
\c 12
\cd By the foreſaid examples he exhorteth them to patience, 2 and by example of Chriſt him ſelf crucified, 5 and because this diſcipline is an argument that they be Gods children, 9 vvith vvhoſe rodde they ſhould be much more content then with that of their carnal fathers: and becauſe it bringeth iuſtification. 12 Exhorting them therfore to plucke vp their hartes, and to take faſter footing: 18 conſidering that all being novv ſo ſweete, and not terrible as in the old Teſtament, their damnation, if they refuſe to heare, wil be ſo much the greater.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* therfore vve alſo hauing ſo great a cloud of vvitneſſes put vpon vs: \x - \xt Col. 3, 8.\x* laying avvay al vveight and ſinne that compaſſeth vs, by patience let vs runne to the fight propoſed vnto vs,
\v 2 looking on the author of faith, and the conſummator \sc IESVS\sc*, vvho, ioy being propoſed vnto him, ſuſtained the croſſe, contemning confuſion, and ſitteth on the right hand of the ſeate of God.
\v 3 For, thinke diligently vpon him vvhich ſuſtained of ſinners ſuch contradiction againſt him ſelf: that you be not vvearied, fainting in your mindes.
\v 4 For you haue not yet reſiſted vnto bloud, repugning againſt ſinne:
\v 5 and you haue forgotten the conſolation, vvhich ſpeaketh to you, as it vvere to children, ſaying, \x - \xt Prov. 3, 11. Apoc. 3, 19.\x* \it My ſonne, neglect not the diſcipline of our Lord: neither be thou vvearied vvhiles thou art rebuked of him\it*.
\v 6 \x - \xt 1. Pet. 2, 1.\x* \it For vvhom our Lord loueth, he chaſteneth: and he ſcourgeth euery childe that he receiueth\it*, \ef - \fr 12.6 \fq He ſcourgeth. \ft ¹ By this we proue that God often puniſheth the ſinnes euen of his louing children, though not with eternal damnation, yet vvith temporall chaſtiſement and correction: & that he doth not alwaies together vvith the remiſſion of deadly ſinnes & eternal puniſhment, exempt the offender receiued to his grace, from al fatherly correction either in this life or in the next. Neither haue the Heretikes of this time any reaſon or ſcripture in the world, vvhy they ſhould take avvay Gods chaſtiſement of his children in the next life, more then in this world. \fq ¹ \ft Temporal punishment after remission of sinnes, either here, or in purgatorie.\ef*
\v 7 Perſeuêre ye in diſcipline. As vnto children doth God offer him ſelf to you. for vvhat ſonne is there, vvhom the father doth not correct?
\v 8 But if you be vvithout diſcipline, vvhereof al be made partakers: then are you baſtards, & not children.
\v 9 Moreouer the fathers in deede of our fleſh vve had for inſtructors, and vve did reuerence them: ſhal vve not much more obey the Father of ſpirites, & liue?
\v 10 And they in deede for a time of fevv daies, according to their vvil inſtructed vs: but he, to that vvhich is profitable in receiuing of his ſanctification.
\v 11 And al diſcipline for the preſent certes ſeemeth not to be of ioy, but of ſorovv: but aftervvard it vvil render to them that are exerciſed by it, moſt peaceable fruite of iuſtice.
\v 12 For the vvhich cauſe ſtretch vp the ſlacked handes and the looſe knees:
\v 13 and make ſtraight ſteppes to your feete: that no man halting erre, but rather be healed.
\v 14 \x - \xt Rom. 12, 18.\x* Folovv peace vvith al men, and holineſſe: vvithout vvhich no man ſhal ſee God:
\v 15 looking diligently leſt any man \f 1 \fr 12.15 \ft That we be not good, there is no lacke on Gods part, who offereth his grace to vs: but the defect is in our ſelues that are not anſwerable to Gods calling of vs and grace towards vs.\f* be wanting to the grace of God: leſt any roote of bitternes ſpringing vp do hinder, and by it many be polluted.
\v 16 Leſt there be any fornicator or prophane perſon \x - \xt Gen. 25, 33.\x* as Eſau: \f 2 \fr 12.16 \ft Such as forſake their ſaluation and religion to ſaue their lands and goods, are like Eſau. · Gen. 25, 33.\f* vvho for one diſh of meate ſold his firſt-birth-rightes.
\v 17 For know ye that aftervvard alſo deſiring to inherite the benediction, he vvas reprobated: \x - \xt Gen. 27, 38.\x* for he found not place of repentance, although vvith teares he had ſought it. \ef - \fr 12.17 \fq He found not. \ft It is not meant, that Eſau could not find remiſſion of his ſinne at Gods hand: but that, hauing once ſold and yelded vp the right of his firſt-birth to his yonger brother, it vvas to late to be ſorie for his vnaduiſed bargaine.\ef*
\v 18 For you are not come to \x - \xt Exo. 19, 20.\x* a palpable mount, and an \f 2 \fr 12.18 \fq `acceſſible \ft \+it `kindled\+it* (or) \+it burning\+it*.\f* `acceſſible' fire, and vvhirlevvinde, and darkenes, and ſtorme,
\v 19 and the ſound of trompet, & voice of vvordes, vvhich they that heard, excuſed them ſelues, that the vvord might not be ſpokē to them,
\v 20 ( for they did not beare that which was ſaid, \x - \xt Exo. 19, 12.\x* \it And if a beaſt ſhal touche the mount, it ſhal be ſtoned\it*.
\v 21 And ſo terrible vvas it vvhich vvas ſeen, Moyſes ſaid: \it I am frighted and tremble\it*.
\v 22 But \f 1 \fr 12.22 \ft The faithful are made fellowes of Angels & of al the perfect ſoules departed ſince the beginning of the world, and of Chriſt him ſelf.\f* you are come to mount Sion, and the citie of the liuing God, heauenly Hieruſalem, and the aſſemblie of many thouſand Angels,
\v 23 & the Church of the firſt-borne, vvhich are vvritten in the heauens, and the iudge of all, God: and the ſpirites of the iuſt \f 1 \fr 12.23 \ft c τετελειωμένων.\f* made perfect,
\v 24 and the mediator of the nevv Teſtament \sc IESVS\sc*, and the ſprinkling of bloud ſpeaking better then \x - \xt Gen. 4, 10.\x* Abel.
\v 25 See that you refuſe him not ſpeaking. for if they eſcaped not, refuſing him that ſpake vpon the earth: much more vve, that turne avvay from him ſpeaking to vs from heauen.
\v 26 VVhoſe voice moued the earth then: but novv he promiſeth, ſaying, \x - \xt Ag. 2, 7.\x* \it Yet once: and I vvil moue not only the earth, but heauen alſo\it*.
\v 27 And in that he ſaieth, Yet once, he declareth the tranſlation of moueable things as being made, that thoſe things may remaine vvhich are vnmoueable.
\v 28 Therfore receiuing an vnmoueable kingdom, vve haue grace: by the vvhich \f 1 \fr 12.28 \ft c λατρεύωμεν.\f* let vs ſerue pleaſing God, vvith feare & reuerence.
\v 29 For \x - \xt Deu. 4, 24.\x* our God is a conſuming fire.
\c 13
\cd He commandeth vnto them mutual loue, 2 hoſpitality, 3 compaſſion, 4 chaſtitie, 5 contentation, 7 imitation of the faith of their Catholike Prelates and Martyrs (not harkening to the doctrines of Heretikes, nor fearing the caſting out of the Iewes ſynagogue) 17 and obedience to their preſent paſtors. 18 And ſo with requeſting their praiers, and praying for them, he endeth the Epiſtle.
\p
\v 1 \sc LET\sc* \x - \xt Rom. 12, 10. 1. Pet. 4.\x* \f 2 \fr 13.1 \ft c ἡ φιλαδελφία.\f* the charitie of the fraternitie abide in you.
\v 2 And hoſpitalitie do not forget, for by this, certaine being not avvare, \x - \xt Gen. 18, 3. 19, 2. 3.\x* haue receiued Angels to harbour. \ef - \fr 13.2 \fq Hoſpitality. \ft ¹ Hoſpitality, that is, receiuing and harbouring of poore pilgrimes, perſecuted and deſolate perſons, is ſo acceptable to God and ſo honorable, that oftentimes it hath been mens good happe to ² harbour Angels in ſteede of poore folke vnawares. Vvhich muſt needes be euer a great benediction to them and their families, as we ſee by Abraham and Lot \+it Gen. 18. & 19\+it*. (and the like fell alſo to S. Gregorie, as Io. Diaconus vvriteth, to whoſe ordinarie table of poore men, not onely Angels but Chriſt alſo came in Pilgrimes weede. \+it In vit. li. 1. c. 10. & li. 2. c. 22, 23\+it*.) vvherof if we had not example and vvarrant by S. Paules vvordes in this place, and many other expreſſe Scriptures of the old Teſtament, theſe ſcorneful miſcreants of this time making ſo litle account both of good vvorkes and ſuch miraculous enterance of Chriſt and his Angels into holy mens harbour, would make this alſo ſeeme fabulous, as they do other like things. \fq ¹ \ft Hospitalitie. \fq ² \ft Angels harboured.\ef*
\v 3 Remember them in bondes, as if you vvere bound vvith them: and them that labour, as your ſelues alſo remaining in bodie.
\v 4 Mariage honorable in all, and the bed vndefiled. For, fornicatours & aduouterers God vvil iudge. \ef - \fr 13.4 \fq Mariage honorable. \ft ¹ \+it The Apoſtle\+it* (ſaith a holy doctor) \+it ſaith, Mariage honorable in all, and the bed vndefiled. And therfore the ſeruants of God in that they are not maried, thinke not the good of mariage to be a fault, but yet they doubt not perpetual continencie to be better then good mariage, ſpecially in this time when it is ſaid of continencie, He that can take, let him take. De fid. ad Pet. c. 3. apud Aug. in fine\+it*. Marke the doctrine of the fathers and of the Catholike Church concerning matrimonie, that it is honorable, and ſo honorable, that it is a holy ſacrament, but yet ² inferiour to virginity and perpetual continencie: honorable in all, that is, all ſuch as may lawfully marie and are lawfully maried: not in brother and ſiſter, not in perſons that haue vowed the contrarie, to whom the ſame Apoſtle ſaith it is damnable. \+it 1. Tim. 5. v. 11\+it*. And this were the meaning of this place, if it vvere to be read thus, \+it Mariage is honorable\+it*. ³ But to ſee how the Proteſtants in all their tranſlations, to abuſe the ſimple, do falſifie this ſentence of the Apoſtle, to make it ſerue for the mariage of Votaries, it is notorious. Firſt, they vſe deceit in ſupplying the verbe ſubſtantiue that wanteth, making it the Indicatiue moode thus, \+it Mariage is honorable &c\+it*. ⁴ as though the Apoſtle affirmed al mariage to be honorable or lawful, where the verbe to be ſupplied ought rather to be the Imperatiue moode, \+it Let mariage be honorable\+it*, that ſo the ſpeache may be an exhortation or commaundement to them that be or wil be maried, to vſe them ſelues in that ſtate in al fidelity, cleanlineſſe, and coniugal continencie one toward an other: as when S. Peter ⁵ alſo and this Apoſtle ⁶ exhorte maried men to giue honour to their wiues as to the vveaker veſſels, and to poſſeſſe their veſſel in honour, not in the paſſions of ignominie and vncleanlineſſe: this is honorable or chaſt mariage, to which he here exhorteth. And that it is rather an exhortation, then an affirmation, it is euident by the other partes and circumſtances of this place both before & after: al vvhich are exhortations in their owne tranſlations. this only being in the middes, and as indifferent to be an exhortation as the reſt (by their owne confeſſion) they reſtraine of purpoſe. Our text therfore and al Catholike tranſlations leaue the ſentence indifferent ⁷ as it is in the Greeke, and as true tranſlatours ought to do, not preſuming to addict it to one ſide, leſt they ſhould reſtraine the ſenſe of the holy Ghoſt to their owne particular fantaſie. Againe, ⁸ our new Tranſlatours corrupt the text in that they tranſlate, \+it in omnibus, among al men\+it*, becauſe ſo they thinke it would ſound better to the ignorant, that Prieſts, Religious, and al vvhoſoeuer, may marie: vvhere they can not tell either by the Greeke or Latin, that \+it in omnibus\+it* ſhould be the maſculine gendre, rather then the neutre (as not only Eraſmus, but ⁹ the Greeke doctors alſo take it) to ſignifie that mariage ſhould be honorably kept betweene man and vvife in al pointes and in al reſpectes. \+it See S. Chryſ. and Theophyl. in hunc locum\+it*. For there may be many filthy abuſes in wedlocke, vvhich the Apoſtle vvarneth them to take heede of, and to keepe their mariage-bed vndefiled. But the third corruption for their purpoſe aforeſaid, and moſt impudent, is, ¹⁰ that ſome of the Caluiniſtes for, \+it in omnibus\+it*, tranſlate, \+it inter quoſuis\+it*, vvith a marginal interpretation to ſignifie al orders, conditions, ſtates, and qualities of men. So boldly they take away al indifferencie of ſenſes, and make Gods word to ſpeake iuſt that which them ſelues would, and their hereſie requireth, in vvhich kind they paſſe al impudencie and al heretikes that euer were. \fq ¹ \ft How mariage is honorable in al, if the Apostle did so say, as he doth not. \fq ² \ft \+it 1 Cor. 7. v. 38\+it*. \fq ³ \ft One short place manifoldly corrupted by the Protestants. \fq ⁴ \ft They restraine the sense to their Heretical fansie. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it 1 Pet. 3\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it 1 Thes. 4\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it τίμιος ὁ γάμος ἐν πᾶσι\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it The Eng. Bib. 1577\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Oecum. in Collect\+it*. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Beza in no. Test. Græcolat. an. 1565\+it*.\ef*
\v 5 Let your maners be vvithout auarice: contented vvith things preſent. For he ſaid, \x - \xt Deu. 31. Ios. 1.\x* \it I vvil not leaue thee, neither vvil I forſake thee\it*.
\v 6 ſo that vve do confidently ſay: \x - \xt Pſal. 55, 12. 117, 6.\x* \it Our Lord is my helper: I vvil not feare vvhat man ſhal doe to me\it*.
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 13.7 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Nicolas 6. Decemb. & S. Auguſtin our Apoſtle and for ſome other Confeſſors Biſhops.\f* Remember your Prelates, vvhich haue ſpoken the vvord of God to you: the end of vvhoſe conuerſation beholding, imitate their faith. \ef - \fr 13.7 \fq Remember your Prelates. \ft ¹ We be here vvarned to haue great regard in our life and beleefe, to the holy fathers, Doctors, & glorious Biſhops gone before vs in Gods Church, not doubting but they being our lawful Paſtors, had and taught the truth: of whom S. Auguſtine ſaid, \+it That which they found in the Church, they held faſt: that which they learned, they taught: that which they receiued of their fathers, the ſame they deliuered to their children. Cont. Iulian. li. 2 c. 10\+it*. Which reſpect to our holy forefathers in faith, is now in this vvicked contempt of the Heretikes, ſo much the more to be had. See the ſaid holy doctors ſecond booke againſt Iulian the Pelagian throughout, what great account he maketh of them in the confutation of hereſies, and how far he preferreth them aboue the proud Sectmaiſters of that time: as vve muſt now doe againſt our new doctors. ² This place alſo is rightly vſed to proue that the Church of God ſhould keepe the memories of Saincts departed, by ſolemne holidaies and other deuout vvaies of honour. \fq ¹ \ft We must haue regard to the faith & doctrine of the fathers. \fq ² \ft Memories and feastes of Saincts.\ef*
\v 8 \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt yeſterday, and to day: the ſame alſo for euer.
\v 9 VVith \f 1 \fr 13.9 \ft New, diuers, changeable, & ſtrange doctrines to be auoided, for ſuch be hereticall. Againſt which the beſt remedie or preſeruatiue is, alvvaies to looke backe to our firſt Apoſtles, & the holy fathers doctrine.\f* various & ſtrange doctrines be not led avvay. For it is beſt that the hart be eſtabliſhed vvith grace, not vvith meates: vvhich haue not profited thoſe that vvalke in them. \ef - \fr 13.9 \fq Not vvith meates. \ft ¹ He ſpeaketh not of Chriſtian faſtes, but of the legal difference of meates, which the Hebrues were yet prone vnto: not conſidering that by Chriſtes faith they vvere made free from al ſuch obſeruations of the Law. \fq ¹ \ft Iudaical abstinence from meates.\ef*
\v 10 VVe haue an altar: vvhereof they haue not povver to eate vvhich ſerue the tabernacle. \ef - \fr 13.10 \fq We haue an altar. \ft ¹ He putteth them in minde by theſe vvordes, that in folowing to much their old Iewiſh rites, they depriued them ſelues of an other maner and a more excellent ſacrifice and meate: meaning, of the holy altar, and Chriſtes owne bleſſed body offered and eaten there. of which, they that continue in the figures of the old Law, could not be partakers. \+it This altar\+it* (ſaith Iſychius) \+it is the altar of Chriſtes body, which the Iewes for their incredulity muſt not behold. Li. 6 c. 21 in Leuit\+it*. And the Greeke word ² (as alſo the Hebrue ³ anſwering therevnto in the old teſtament) ſignifieth properly an altar to ſacrifice on, and not a metaphorical and ſpiritual altar. Whereby vve proue againſt the Heretikes, that vve haue not a common table or profane communion borde, to eate mere bread vpon, but a very altar in the proper ſenſe, to ſacrifice Chriſtes body vpon: and ſo called of the fathers in reſpect of the ſaid body ſacrificed. \+it Greg. Nazianz. in orat. de ſorore Gorgonia. Chryſ. demonſt. quod Chriſtus ſit Deus. Socrat. li. 1. c. 20. 25. Aug. ep. 86. De ciu. Dei. li. 8. c. 27. & li. 22. c. 10. Confeſſ. li. 9 c. 11. 13. Cont. Fauſt. Manich. li. 20. c. 21. Theophyl. in 23. Mat\+it*. And when it is called a table, it is in reſpect of the heauenly foode of Chriſts body and bloud receiued. \fq ¹ \ft Material altars for the sacrifice of Christs body. \fq ² \ft \+it θυσιαστήριον.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it מזבח\+it*\ef*
\v 11 For \x - \xt Lev. 16, 27.\x* the bodies of thoſe beaſtes, vvhoſe bloud for ſinne is caried into the holies by the high prieſt, are burned vvithout the campe.
\v 12 For the vvhich thing \sc IESVS\sc* alſo, that he might ſanctifie the people by his ovvne bloud, ſuffered vvithout the gate.
\v 13 Let vs goe forth therfore to him vvithout the campe: carying his reproche.
\v 14 For vve haue not here a permanent citie: but vve ſeeke that vvhich is to come.
\v 15 By him therfore let vs offer the hoſt of praiſe alvvaies to God, that is to ſay, \x - \xt Oſe. 14, 3.\x* the fruite of lippes confeſſing to his name. \ef - \fr 13.15 \fq The hoſte of praiſe. \ft ¹ Though it may ſignifie the ſpiritual ſacrifices of praiſe and thankeſgiuing of what ſort ſoeuer: yet it ſpecially may be thought to ſignifie the great Sacrifice of the B. body and bloud of Chriſt: not as vpon the Croſſe, vvhich vvas but once done in bloudy ſort, but as in the Church, and new Teſtament, where it is daily done vnbloudily, being the proper hoſt of laude and thankes giuing, and therfore called the \+it Euchariſt\+it*, and being the fruite and effecte of Chriſt and his Prieſts lippes or wordes, that is, of conſecration: becauſe this ſacrifice is made by the force of the holy wordes, And when we reade in the pſalme and other places of the olde Teſtament, of the hoſt of praiſe, it may be thought to be a prophecie of the new Sacrifice, & not of euery vulgar thankes giuing. And ſo the old fathers in the primitiue Church to hide the myſteries from the vnworthy or heathen, often ſpeake. \+it What is\+it* (ſaith S. Auguſtine) \+it a more holy ſacrifice of praiſe, then that which conſiſteth in thankes giuing, all which the faithful do know in the ſacrifice of the Church. Li. 1. cont. aduers. leg. & proph. c. 18\+it*. Againe, \+it c. 20. The Church from the times of the Apoſtles by the moſt certaine ſucceſſions of Biſhops, offereth to God in the body of Chriſt the Sacrifice of praiſe\+it*. And a litle aftervvard, \+it Now Iſraël according to the ſpirit, that is, the Church offereth a ſingular Sacrifice according to the ſpirit: of whoſe houſe he vvil not take calues nor goates, but vvil take the Sacrifice of praiſe, not according to the order of Aaron, but according to the order of Melchiſedec. See ep. 120. c. 19. & ep. 57. ad q. 1. in fine\+it*. Thus you ſee, when the holy fathers handle the Scriptures, they finde Maſſe and Sacrifice in many places, vvhere the ignorant heretikes or the ſimple might thinke they ſpeake onely of a common thankes giuing. \fq ¹ \ft The Sacrifice of the altar is the principal host of praise and thankes giuing, therfore called, \+it Eucharistia\+it*.\ef*
\v 16 And beneficence aud communication do not forget: for vvith ſuch hoſtes God is promerited. \ef - \fr 13.16 \fq God is promerited. \ft ¹ This latin vvord \+it promeretur\+it*, cannot be expreſſed effectually in any one Engliſh word. It ſignifieth, Gods fauour to be procured by the foreſaid vvorkes of almes and charitie, as by the deſerts and merites of the doers. Which doctrine and word of merites the Aduerſaries like ſo il, that they flee both here and els vvhere from the word, tranſlating here for, \+it promeretur Deus, God is pleaſed\+it*, more neere to the Greeke, ²as they pretend. Which in deede maketh no more for them then the latin, vvhich is agreable to moſt auncient copies, as vve ſee by Primaſius S. Auguſtines ſcholer. ³ For if God be pleaſed vvith good workes and ſhew fauour for them, then are they meritorious, and then only faith is not the cauſe of Gods fauour to men. \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants auoid the word merite. \fq ² \ft \+it ἐυαρεστεῖται.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Good workes meritorious.\ef*
\v 17 Obey your Prelates, and be ſubiect to them. For they vvatch as being to render account for your ſoules: ⊢ that they may doe this vvith ioy, and not mourning. for this is not expedient for you. \ef - \fr 13.17 \fq Obey your Prelates. \ft ¹ There is nothing more inculcated in the holy Scriptures, then obedience of the lay people to the Prieſts and Prelates of Gods Church, in matters of ſoule, conſcience, and religion. Whereof the Apoſtle giueth this reaſon, becauſe they haue the charge of mens ſoules, and muſt anſwer for them: vvhich is an infinite preeminence and ſuperiority, ioyned vvith burden, and requireth maruelous ſubmiſsion and moſt obedient ſubiection of al that be vnder them and their gouernement. From this obedience there is no exception nor exemption of kings nor Princes, be they neuer ſo great. If they haue ſoules, and be Chriſtian men, they muſt be ſubiect to ſome Biſhop, Prieſt, or other Prelate. ² And vvhatſoeuer he be (though Emperour of all the world) if he take vpon him to preſcribe and giue lawes of religion to the Biſhops and Prieſts, vvhom he ought to obey and be ſubiect vnto in religion, he ſhal be damned vndoubtedly, except he repent, becauſe he doth againſt the expreſſe vvord of God and law of nature. And by this you may ſee the difference of an heretical and a diſordered time, from other Catholike Chriſtian daies. For hereſie and the like damnable reuoltes from the Church of God, is no more but a rebellion and diſobedience to the Prieſts of Gods Church, when men refuſe to be vnder their diſcipline, to heare their doctrine, and interpretation of Scriptures, to obey their lawes and counſels. This diſobedience and rebellion from the Spiritual Gouernour, vnder pretence of obedience to the Temporal, is the bane of our daies, and ſpecially of our Countrie, where theſe new Sectes are properly mainteined by this falſe principle, That the Prince in matters of ſoule and religion may commaund the Prelate: vvhich is directly and euidently againſt this Scripture and all other, that commaund the ſheepe of Chriſtes fold to obey their ſpiritual Officers. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle doth inculcate obedience to the Priests and Bishops of Gods Church. \fq ² \ft No person exempted from this obedience, in matters of religion.\ef*
\v 18 Pray for vs. for vve haue confidence that vve haue a good conſcience, vvilling to conuerſe vvel in all.
\v 19 And I beſeeche you the more to doe this, that I may the more ſpedily be reſtored to you.
\v 20 And the God of peace vvhich brought out from the dead the great Paſtor of the ſheepe, in the bloud of the eternal teſtament, our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt:
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 13.21 \ft \+it καταρτίσαι\+it*, that is, \+it make you perfect and abſolute in al goodnes\+it*.\f*\f 2 \fr 13.21 \ft \+it aptet vos\+it*.\f* cfitte you in al goodnes, that you may doe his vvil, doing in you that vvhich may pleaſe before him by \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt: to vvhom is glorie for euer and euer. Amen.
\v 22 And I deſire you brethren that you ſuffer the vvord of conſolation. For in very fevv vvordes haue I vvritten to you.
\v 23 Knovv you our brother Timothee to be diſmiſſed: vvith vvhom (if he come the ſooner) I vvil ſee you.
\v 24 Salute al your prelates, and al the ſainctes. The brethren of Italie ſalute you.
\v 25 Grace be vvith you al. Amen.
